\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\b \uc2 KELLOGG'S\uc2  WORKERS VOTE TO END STRIKE WILL RETURN TO WORK AFTER AGREEING TO NEW CONTRACT\b0\par\par\uc2 321 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 December 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STARK WINTER WARNING\par \par \uc2 OMICRON, CDC, SURGE, TESTING, RAUNER, WOLFE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, COVID, UNVACCINATED, OMICRON, TESTS\par \par \uc2 OMICRON, OLYMPICS, NHL, SURGE, \uc2 IOC\par \par \uc2 WALENSKY, OMICRON, TESTING, HOLIDAYS, BOOSTER, PILL\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, OMICRON, \uc2 FAA\uc2 , UNRULY, DELTA\par \par \uc2 STORM, RAIN, SNOW, MUDSLIDES\par \par \uc2 KELLOGG'S\uc2 , STRIKE, UNION, EMPLOYEES, CONTRACT\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Wow. All right, good to know and to plan ahead for it. Ginger, thank you very much. Turning now to \uc2 Kellogg's\uc2  workers ending their 11-week-long strike after ratifying a tentative new contract with the cereal giant. Let's go to Alex Perez with all of the latest details for us. Good morning, Alex.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 KELLOGG'S\uc2  WORKERS VOTE TO END STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILL RETURN TO WORK NEXT WEEK AFTER AGREEING TO NEW CONTRACT\par \par \uc2 ALEX PEREZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Hey, good morning, Amy. After weeks of back and forth and the company even threatening to replace those striking workers, a deal has finally been reached. Now the union representing some 1,400 \uc2 Kellogg's\uc2  employees and the company have reached a new five-year agreement. The new deal gives employees at the cereal and snack making company new health benefits and a cost of living raise at that \uc2 Kellogg's\uc2  four plants in Michigan, Nebraska, Tennessee and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PEREZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Veteran employees get $1.10 raise, with new hires now making $24.11 an hour. And, Amy, the new deal also guarantees none of the company's plants will be closed for at least the next five years. Amy, guys.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CELEBRATING THE HOLIDAYS SAFELY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT MEDICAL EXPERTS RECOMMEND TO LOWER YOUR RISK\par \par \uc2 AMY ROBACH (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, Alex Perez, thank you. Coming up next, tips from medical experts on reducing your risk of COVID when you gather for the holidays. But first let's go back to Ginger.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020220112ehcm00030\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 QUITTING WITH "QUIT-TOK" THE GREAT RESIGNATION HITS SOCIAL MEDIA\b0\par\par\uc2 810 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 December 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 OMICRON, COVID-19, WALENSKY, BOURLA, FAUCI, SANTUCCI\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, OMICRON, UNITED KINGDOM, MANDATES\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINE, MANDATES, MEADOWS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 TRIAL, POTTER, WRIGHT, BRYANT, ENGH, ELDRIDGE\par \par \uc2 HOLMES, TRIAL, THERANOS\par \par \uc2 SPACE, TRAVEL, BLUE ORIGIN, DELAY, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BLUE ORIGIN LAUNCH DELAYED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WIND CONDITIONS PUSH MICHAEL'S LIFTOFF TO SATURDAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 CANCUN, JONES, SHOOTING, ALMONTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TROUBLE IN PARADISE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AUTHORITIES ON ALERT AS GUNFIRE BREAKS OUT ON CANCUN BEACH AGAIN\par \par \uc2 MURDER, INVESTIGATION, PIERS, DELPHI\par \par \uc2 WOODS, COMEBACK, GOLF\par \par \uc2 GIFT CARDS, SAFETY, SCAMS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GIFT CARD SCAM ALERT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WARNING ABOUT COMMON SCAMS & HOW TO AVOID GETTING DUPED\par \par \uc2 TRICK SHOT, ZEKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 OMICRON, COVID-19, WALENSKY, VACCINES, BOURLA\par \par \uc2 SPACE TRAVEL, BLUE ORIGIN, WIND\par \par \uc2 "BEING THE RICARDOS", RODRIGO, FERRELL\par \par \uc2 "AND JUST LIKE THAT", PARKER, NIXON, DAVIS, JACKSON\par \par \uc2 ANXIETY, DEPRESSION, YOUTH, PARENTING\par \par \uc2 RESIGN, QUITTOK, MAYES, KNIGHTEN, WONG, IANNIELLO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: QUITTING WITH "QUIT-TOK"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE GREAT RESIGNATION HITS SOCIAL MEDIA\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We turn now to the great resignation, the number of Americans quitting their jobs remaining near a record in October.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And now, some people are taking to social media as they leave, sharing quittoks and making the decision public. We are gonna squeeze one more story out of Rebecca Jarvis this morning. She is back now with this growing trend.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Don't quit on us.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Rebecca, we can't do it without you. So, please don't quit.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I don't quit, T.J. But it is quitting time for millions of Americans, voluntarily opting out. Happening in every industry, but nowhere more prevalent than in retail and leisure and hospitality. And now, some are turning to quit-toks to seal the deal.\par \par \uc2 CONTENT CREATOR (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 But I need some time off the hamster wheel to focus on myself and to make an impact in my community, so I quit my job.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's part of the great resignation happening all across the country. But in this case, it's happening on TikTok. They're called quittoks. Users like Marisa Mayes and Tiffany Knighten announcing publicly on the social media platform that they're leaving their jobs.\par \par \uc2 MARISSA MAYES (CONTENT CREATOR TIKTOK)\par \par \uc2 This is me 15 minutes after quitting my corporate job.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) They say quitting their day jobs was all about finding happiness and focusing on mental health.\par \par \uc2 MARISSA MAYES (CONTENT CREATOR TIKTOK)\par \par \uc2 I kind of was faced with a decision like, okay, you either need to commit to what you're doing or you need to get out now because you're not happy at all.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY KNIGHTEN (CONTENT CREATOR TIKTOK)\par \par \uc2 Dealing with microaggressions in the workplace and inequality and pay disparities, and I chose myself and prioritizing my mental health.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) According to the Department of Labor, they're not alone, 4.2 million Americans quit their jobs in October, bringing the total number just this year to 38.6 million people voluntarily quitting, a record high.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SUTHERLAND-WONG (CEO GLASSDOOR)\par \par \uc2 On Glassdoor's platform, we've seen more than 100% increase in mentions of the word burnout in reviews that we see.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For Gabby Ianniello, going public with her resignation helped her launch a podcast career under the name Corporate Quitter for those aiming to follow a similar path.\par \par \uc2 GABBY IANNIELLO (CONTENT CREATOR TIKTOK/HOST "CORPORATE QUITTER" PODCAST)\par \par \uc2 I was like, well, why don't I be that person then to kind of share my story, right? Share the struggle, share when I don't have money or when the budgeting is tight and share the wins.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Experts warn, the message quittoks send could be misinterpreted.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTIAN SUTHERLAND-WONG (CEO GLASSDOOR)\par \par \uc2 You worry at times that people could be burning relationships and burning existing relationships or set themselves up for problems with relationships in the future.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Which is, of course, the big caveat here. This is social media. It lives on forever. You want a long career. And the economy, which is in excellent shape for job seekers right now, it can ebb and flow. So, if you're gonna go this route, keep it respectful, thoughtful. You don't wanna turn off a future employer and sit down and have a chat with them first, T.J., before you post it on TikTok.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah, some good advice right there.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Heads-up.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That was directed.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Rebecca, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211228ehc900067\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 KELLOGG'S WORKERS REJECT CONTRACT PROPOSAL COMPANY TO HIRE PERMANENT REPLACEMENTS\b0\par\par\uc2 321 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 December 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SHOTS VERSUS OMICRON\par \par \uc2 OMICRON, \uc2 PFIZER\uc2 , EFFICACY, BOURLA, COVID, SURGE\par \par \uc2 JHA, COVID, OMICRON, VACCINE, BOOSTER, HOLIDAYS\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, PUTIN, MEETING, UKRAINE, SULLIVAN\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, UKRAINE, BORDER, TENSION, TROOPS\par \par \uc2 DEBT CEILING, DEFAULT, HOUSE, DEAL, MEADOWS\par \par \uc2 POTTER, WRIGHT\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, \uc2 KELLOGG'S\uc2 , UNION, REPLACEMENT\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Stephanie Ramos, thanks. We're gonna turn now to the strike at \uc2 Kellogg's\uc2  entering its third month, and \uc2 Kellogg's\uc2  is now replacing more than a thousand workers after the union rejected the latest offer from the cereal giant. Alex Perez has the details. Good morning, Alex.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 KELLOGG'S\uc2  WORKERS REJECT CONTRACT PROPOSAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMPANY TO HIRE PERMANENT REPLACEMENTS AS STRIKE ENTERS THIRD MONTH\par \par \uc2 ALEX PEREZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Hey, good morning, George. The 1,400 workers have been on strike since October, and since a deal has not been reached, \uc2 Kellogg's\uc2  says it's moving forward with plans to find permanent replacements for those workers. Now, the striking employees have rejected a five-year contract offer, which would have provided 3% raises and maintained current health care benefits at \uc2 Kellogg's\uc2  four plants in Michigan, Nebraska, Pennsylvania, and Tennessee. The union employees who turned down the deal say they won't return to work until \uc2 Kellogg's\uc2  stops a two-tiered system of wages, which gives newer workers less pay and fewer benefits.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PEREZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) In a statement the President of Kellogg's North America says, "We must take the necessary steps to ensure business continuity. We have an obligation to our customers and consumers to continue to provide the cereals that they know and love." And in another twist here, labor experts say they don't believe \uc2 Kellogg's\uc2  will be able to find the number of people it needs to replace those workers, so all of this is far from over. T.J.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211228ehc800008\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE FUTURE OF WORK FROM HOME NEW BOOK'S TIPS FOR RETHINKING REMOTE WORKPLACE\b0\par\par\uc2 886 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 December 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON ALERT\par \par \uc2 COVID, OMICRON, VACCINE, WALENSKY, MCGINN, FAUCI\par \par \uc2 ARCTIC, WIND, SNOW, ICE\par \par \uc2 RUSSIA, UKRAINE, TROOPS, \uc2 NATO\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, PUTIN, UKRAINE, CHINA, OLYMPICS\par \par \uc2 CUOMO, CNN, GOVERNOR, ALLEGATIONS\par \par \uc2 VOLCANO, ERUPTION, ASH, INDONESIA, JAVA\par \par \uc2 DOLE, SENATE, WORLD WAR II, TRIBUTES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HAWAII RADAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, CRUMBLEY, SCHOOL\par \par \uc2 MCDONALD, SHOOTING, VICTIMS, PARENTS, CHARGES, GUN\par \par \uc2 COUNTDOWN, SPACE, BLUE ORIGIN, SPROGE, KNIGHTS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, OMICRON, WALENSKY, FAUCI, MCGINN\par \par \uc2 BLOOD PRESSURE, SPIKE, PANDEMIC, WOMEN, TIPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BLOOD PRESSURE SPIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW STUDY FINDS RISE AMONG ADULTS DURING PANDEMIC, ESPECIALLY WOMEN\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , \uc2 DETROIT LIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LONG LAST!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LIONS SNAP 364-DAY WINLESS STREAK WITH DRAMATIC VICTORY\par \par \uc2 SPACE, TRAVEL, BLUE ORIGIN, PHELPS, TRAINING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MICHAEL'S LIFTOFF TO SPACE\par \par \uc2 DIABETES, SHELTON, PAGLIUCA, STEM CELL, STUDY\par \par \uc2 INFLATION, ECONOMY, SHOPPING, TIPS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BEAT INFLATION WHILE HOLIDAY SHOPPING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIPS FOR STRETCHING YOUR MONEY & SAVING WHILE SPENDING\par \par \uc2 TIKTOK, POWERED BY JOY, GRAZ, BERVELL, JOSEPH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOP TIKTOK VIDEOS OF 2021\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BEST & BUZZIEST TRENDS OF THE YEAR\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 "A VERY BOY BAND HOLIDAY", LACHEY, KIRKPATRICK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DREW LACHEY & CHRIS KIRKPATRICK LIVE!\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CELEBRATING "A VERY BOY BAND HOLIDAY"\par \par \uc2 WORK, HOME, COVID, BOOK, "OUT OF OFFICE", WARZEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE FUTURE OF WORK FROM HOME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW BOOK'S TIPS FOR RETHINKING REMOTE WORKPLACE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now the future of work from home. \uc2 Google\uc2  recently canceled their return to office plans as we learn more about the Omicron variant. And there's a new book called "Out of Office," which is all about revamping our work-from-home situations. Becky Worley took a look. Hey, Becky.\par \par \uc2 BECKY WORLEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Hey, George. Good morning. When the pandemic hit, working from home was a fast pivot in an emergency situation, and now here we are, 18 months later. So maybe it's time to take a hard look at the best ways to re-invent your work-from-home life.\par \par \uc2 BECKY WORLEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The boundaries between work and home seem to have disappeared as we juggle responsibilities 24/7.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE WARZEL ("OUT OF OFFICE")\par \par \uc2 A lot of people compare it to not working from home but living at work, which is obviously not a great place to be.\par \par \uc2 BECKY WORLEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) In 2017, Charlie Warzel and Anne Petersen traded the hustle and bustle of New York City for a slower pace out west. When the pandemic hit, they saw an opportunity to deeply research what does and doesn't work when you're remote.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE WARZEL ("OUT OF OFFICE")\par \par \uc2 A lot of employers are still treating remote work as a perk that you have to earn, and that's really difficult because it puts people in this mindset that they have to be constantly on and overperforming just to deserve this.\par \par \uc2 BECKY WORLEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Their new book, "Out of Office," offers concrete steps to rethink the remote workplace. First, an audit, asking hard questions like...\par \par \uc2 ANNE HELEN PETERSEN ("OUT OF OFFICE")\par \par \uc2 When do I actually start working? People tell lies to themselves all the time about the fact that they sit down to work at 9:00 am, when they actually start working at 6:30 in the morning.\par \par \uc2 BECKY WORLEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Once you know when you work...\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE WARZEL ("OUT OF OFFICE")\par \par \uc2 How long does it take me to get my work for the week done? Like, we surveyed over 700 different people, and all of them at some point confessed really, I get most of my work done in a five-hour sprint.\par \par \uc2 BECKY WORLEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If you want to keep working remotely...\par \par \uc2 ANNE HELEN PETERSEN ("OUT OF OFFICE")\par \par \uc2 Talk to your manager and to highlight all the ways you have been more productive in terms of, like, "I found this to be a really sustainable way to do really high quality work. So how can we figure out the best times when I need to be in the office."\par \par \uc2 BECKY WORLEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And remember that harried mom we showed you juggling it all as she started her day, that's my producer, Amanda, who was on the Zoom call with us, too. When we checked in to make sure everything recorded okay...\par \par \uc2 BECKY WORLEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Who is this that we're looking at? Who's this cutie?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA ("GOOD MORNING AMERICA" PRODUCER)\par \par \uc2 This is Jason.\par \par \uc2 BECKY WORLEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) This is Jason, aww. Look at this, oh my gosh. Work from home warrior.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA ("GOOD MORNING AMERICA" PRODUCER)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, this is the reality of it.\par \par \uc2 BECKY WORLEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Even in this interview.\par \par \uc2 BECKY WORLEY (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And that says it all, no separation between work and family. The key, they say, is more intentionality, recognizing, "Okay, this started in a panic, but I can take a minute with my boss and my family and maybe my son on my lap for Amanda to figure out how to tweak my routines and make things better for everyone," George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That makes a lot of sense. Okay, Becky, thanks very much. "Out of Office: The Big Problem and Bigger Promise of Working from Home" is out tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211223ehc60003h\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 JOHN DEERE STRIKE ENDS DEAL REACHED AFTER WORKERS APPROVE NEW CONTRACT\b0\par\par\uc2 518 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 November 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ARBERY, TRIAL, MCMICHAEL, DUNIKOSKI, TESTIMONY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOMBSHELL TESTIMONY IN AHMAUD ARBERY TRIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEFENDANT WHO SHOT & KILLED 25-YEAR-OLD TAKES THE STAND\par \par \uc2 RITTENHOUSE, TRIAL, CHIRAFISI, SCHROEDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEFENSE SEEKS MISTRIAL IN KYLE RITTENHOUSE TRIAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACCUSE PROSECUTORS OF OVERREACH AS JURY REVIEWS KEY EVIDENCE\par \par \uc2 ARBERY, MCMICHAEL, RITTENHOUSE\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, CASES, SURGE, BOOSTER, VACCINE, FLU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TROUBLING TREND\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CASES & HOSPITALIZATIONS UP; DEATHS NO LONGER EXPECTED TO DECLINE\par \par \uc2 HARRIS, INFLATION, IMMIGRATION, BIDEN, ROLE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, "RUST", MITCHELL, BALDWIN, LAWSUIT, ALLRED\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW ACCUSATION IN FATAL "RUST" FILM SET SHOOTING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SCRIPT SUPERVISOR SAYS ALEC BALDWIN NEVER SHOULD HAVE FIRED GUN\par \par \uc2 MALCOLM X, AZIZ, ISLAM, SHANIES, SCHECK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STUNNING REVERSAL IN MALCOLM X CASE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TWO MEN ACCUSED OF KILLING CIVIL RIGHTS LEADER TO BE EXONERATED\par \par \uc2 SHUAI, GAOLI, SEXUAL ASSAULT, COOK, TENNIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MYSTERY DEEPENS OVER MISSING TENNIS STAR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ALLEGED EMAIL CASTS DOUBTS AFTER SEXUAL ASSAULT ALLEGATIONS\par \par \uc2 TURPIN, "ESCAPE FROM A HOUSE OF HORROR"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "ESCAPE FROM A HOUSE OF HORROR"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW FIRST LOOK AT DIANE SAWYER'S INTERVIEW WITH TWO TURPIN CHILDREN\par \par \uc2 HERRO\par \par \uc2 STORY2\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STRIKE OVER\par \par \uc2 JOHN DEERE, STRIKE, CONTRACT, LABOR\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN DEERE STRIKE ENDS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEAL REACHED AFTER WORKERS APPROVE NEW CONTRACT\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 But first we're going to begin with the John Deere strike ending. More than 10,000 workers walked off the job over a month ago demanding better pay. Gio Benitez joins us with more on their new contract. Good morning, Gio.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Hey, Robin, good morning. Yeah, this is a massive win for those John Deere employees. They waited it out for a month until they got the third contract offer. Now those 10,000 workers will return to work. Let's take a look at that new contract right now. They're going to get 10 percent raises immediately, 20 percent over the lifetime of the contract, essentially doubling their raises, an $8,500 signing bonus, return of cost-of-living adjustments, dramatically improved pension plan options, and workers will be eligible for health insurance a lot sooner.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now, overnight the union saying in a statement, "Our members' courageous willingness to strike in order to attain a better standard of living and a more secure retirement resulted in a groundbreaking contract and sets a new standard for workers not only within the \uc2 UAW\uc2  but throughout the country," Robin.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And many people were watching this, and it really sends a strong message about the economy here in America.\par \par \uc2 GIO BENITEZ (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Oh absolutely, because, remember, Robin, there is still a huge worker shortage across the country right now. That's what was happening with these John Deere employees. They were doing a lot more work during the pandemic, so a lot of experts believe that many companies, they're just going to have to increase raises in order to keep all of their employees.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, Gio, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211208ehbi0000g\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WORKERS QUIT IN RECORD NUMBERS 4.4 MILLION AMERICANS LEAVE THEIR JOBS JUST IN SEPTEMBER\b0\par\par\uc2 556 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 November 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 CAPITOL RIOT, INVESTIGATION, BANNON, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 TRIAL, RITTENHOUSE, SCHROEDER, KRAUS, CHARGES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LESSER CHARGES CONSIDERED\par \par \uc2 SPEARS, ROSENGART, CONSERVATORSHIP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JUDGE ENDS CONSERVATORSHIP\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, GHALY, HOTEZ, MENDENHALL, GILPIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE STATES BACK BOOSTERS\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, JOBS, INFLATION, PSAKI, ZANDI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORKERS QUIT IN RECORD NUMBERS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We're going to turn now to the American economy and jobs, tonight, and a record number revealed today involving the number of Americans walking away from their jobs, 4.4 million American workers quitting in September. So, what's driving this? And this need for workers across the country, of course, playing into this. Inflation now gripping the country. Here's Rachel Scott.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 4.4 MILLION AMERICANS LEAVE THEIR JOBS JUST IN SEPTEMBER\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, the White House on the defensive amid the news that a record 4.4 million Americans quit their jobs in September. Press Secretary \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  putting a rosy spin on the numbers.\par \par \uc2 JEN PSAKI\uc2  (WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY)\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, that's a good thing. Many workers feel this is a time to look for a better job with greater pay and more benefits.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LEAVING WORKFORCE\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Experts say that's not the only reason so many people are quitting. There's also the difficulty of finding childcare during the pandemic, a mismatch between skills and available jobs, and a large number of retirements. The government also surged support for Americans during the pandemic, stimulus checks, enhanced unemployment, rental assistance and food subsidies. But the bottom line is that businesses across the country are desperately searching for workers.\par \par \uc2 BUSINESS OWNER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 I could easily here hire at least another 10 people, and I've, it's hard to get even one.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Susan Lim owns the Praline Bakery and Restaurant in Maryland. Because she can't find workers, she's been forced to curtail her offerings. She can't seat as many customers. What's more, the cost of ingredients is skyrocketing.\par \par \uc2 BUSINESS OWNER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 A case of butter, you know, increased like $15 from last week to this week. And, you know, eggs sometimes can jump $20 from week to week.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Twenty dollars.\par \par \uc2 BUSINESS OWNER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Moody's chief analyst \uc2 Mark Zandi\uc2  says what we're seeing is a perfect storm.\par \par \uc2 MARK ZANDI (CHIEF ECONOMIST MOODY'S ANALYTICS)\par \par \uc2 The worker shortages are conflating with the supply chain problems, just creating this massive shortage of product and, as a result, higher prices, inflation.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Zandi predicts inflation will remain high for more than a year.\par \par \uc2 MARK ZANDI (CHIEF ECONOMIST MOODY'S ANALYTICS)\par \par \uc2 I think were seeing the worst of the inflation right now, but it's not going to get better really fast.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL SCOTT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And economist \uc2 Mark Zandi\uc2  says the fundamental reason for the rise in inflation that we are seeing is the Delta variant. He says it has scrambled the labor markets. He says things will get better, but that it's going to take some time, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Rachel Scott, at the White House, tonight. Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020211115ehbc00006\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HOLLYWOOD WALKOUT AVERTED? STAGE EMPLOYEES' UNION & STUDIOS REACH TENTATIVE AGREEMENT\b0\par\par\uc2 370 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICANS KIDNAPPED\par \par \uc2 KIDNAPPED, MISSIONARIES, HAITI, GANG, FRANKEL\par \par \uc2 KIDNAPPING, MISSIONARIES, RANSOM, NEGOTIATE\par \par \uc2 COVID, VACCINE, MANDATE, LIGHTFOOT, POLICE, PARENTS\par \par \uc2 MISSILE, HYPERSONIC, SECRET, CHINA\par \par \uc2 ARBERY, TRAIL, JURY, MARTIN, MERRITT, JONES\par \par \uc2 SUPPLY, PORTS, SHIPPING, BUTTIGIEG, HACEGABA\par \par \uc2 WNBA, CHAMPIONSHIP, PARKER, CHICAGO, SKY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 CLIP FROM "WHEEL OF FORTUNE"\par \par \uc2 PETITO, SCHMIDT, LAUNDRIE, FUNDRAISER\par \par \uc2 "HALLOWEEN KILLS", BOX OFFICE, MALKIN, CURTIS, "THE BATMAN"\par \par \uc2 STEELE, MEIER, "OUT OF THE SHADOWS"\par \par \uc2 BRAVES, DODGERS, WALK-OFFS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 POWELL, TRIBUTE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GEN. COLIN POWELL DIES AT 84\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE PASSES AWAY FROM COVID COMPLICATIONS\par \par \uc2 HOLLYWOOD, WALKOUT, AGREEMENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLLYWOOD WALKOUT AVERTED?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STAGE EMPLOYEES' UNION & STUDIOS REACH TENTATIVE AGREEMENT\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, we're gonna turn now to the latest on that possible Hollywood strike that threatened to shut down production across the industry.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It has been averted and a new three-year deal was reached between the studios and the union representing film and TV crews. That caught a lot of people's attention. But it looks like that strike has been averted.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC'S NEW HOLIDAY GUIDANCE\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So, coming up here on "GMA," CDC has some holiday guidance. Do we need to worry about winter COVID spikes? Our Dr. Ashton is here.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DAWN STALEY ONE-ON-ONE\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And Dawn Staley making history as the highest-paid woman coach in women's college basketball. She says her groundbreaking contract is worth much more than the money. She's gonna join us live.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JAMIE FOXX IN TIMES SQUARE!\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And Jamie Foxx is here in Times Square this morning. That's all coming up on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We welcome you back to "GMA" as we just shared with you the news about General Powell. We're glad that you are with us on this Monday morning.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And we will have more on that coming up.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We will.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211104ehai0000h\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 MILLENNIALS ON THE MOVE RECORD NUMBER OF AMERICANS QUITTING THEIR JOBS\b0\par\par\uc2 528 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOOSTER BREAKTHROUGH\par \par \uc2 J&J, BOOSTER\par \par \uc2 BOOSTER, VACCINE, MIXING, OFFIT\par \par \uc2 TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 CLINTON, HOSPITALIZED, INFECTION\par \par \uc2 SUPPLY CHAIN, SHORTAGES\par \par \uc2 WHITE HOUSE, TEXAS, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 CAPITOL, INSURRECTION, OFFICER, REILLY\par \par \uc2 POLLACK\par \par \uc2 BEATLES, DOCUMENTARY\par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, VACCINE, MANDATES, BATTLE, BOYCE\par \par \uc2 SNOW, FREEZING\par \par \uc2 MILLENNIALS, CHANGE, CAREERS\par \par \uc2 VICTOR OQUENDO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Welcome back to "GMA" and the shifting labor force. With a record number of Americans, more than 4 million, quitting their jobs in August, leading the charge, millennials. Economic correspondent Deirdre Bolton shows us why.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) More than a year-and-a-half into the pandemic, millions of Americans are changing the way they see their work lives.\par \par \uc2 SARAH FOSTER (ANALYST BANKRATES.COM)\par \par \uc2 They really learned that life is short and that work has to accommodate life, not the other way around. It's not all about, you know, working a 60-hour week, you know, glorifying that. Rest is really important. Work/life balance is really important.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Experts say there is a mental rebalancing for workers of all ages, including millennials.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEARLY 2X AS MANY MILLENNIALS AND GENERATION Z ARE HUNTING FOR A NEW POSITION OR PLANNING TO DO SO OVER THE NEXT 12 MONTHS\par \par \uc2 SARAH FOSTER (ANALYST BANKRATES.COM)\par \par \uc2 Nearly twice as many millennials and even generation Z are hunting for a new position or planning to over the next 12 months, more so than Baby Boomers. Millennials, these younger workers, they're really leading the hunt right now.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) One point worth making, not all workers who are quitting their current jobs are leaving the workforce all together.\par \par \uc2 SARAH FOSTER (ANALYST BANKRATES.COM)\par \par \uc2 A lot of them are quitting their jobs and moving to something that suits them better. So they're looking for things like flexibility and higher pay, and they're using this record demand as leverage. So, this is happening at a time when the labor force is smaller. There is a labor shortage.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If you're looking to change jobs, one recruiter gives us these tips.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIPS FOR FINDING A NEW JOB\par \par \uc2 ROBERT MONROI (CHIEF OPERATING OFFICER CONTRACTRECRUITER.COM)\par \par \uc2 Be more open-minded. Look at companies that you may have not considered before in the past. Have a strong \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  profile. Your employer will likely see your \uc2 LinkedIn\uc2  profile before they'll, they'll see your resume. Don't be afraid to ask pointed questions during your interview. You know, how did your company take care of employees during the pandemic?\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) As for employers, experts tell us the war for talent is real. So, offering remote or flexible work when possible is key to attracting and retaining top talent. Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right. Deirdre, thank so much. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKUPS IN MUSIC\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Still ahead here on "GMA," using music to get through the pain. Adele, man, she's good at that. The latest, she's got that song in a long history of artists. We'll explain.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211029ehag0005x\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 GET ORGANIZED FOR THE FALL HOW TO START PUTTING YOUR HOUSE IN ORDER\b0\par\par\uc2 795 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT CLINTON HOSPITALIZED\par \par \uc2 CLINTON, HOSPITAL, ICU, SEPSIS, ALI\par \par \uc2 VACCINES, BOOSTERS, FDA, BROWNSTEIN, MANDATES\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, VACCINE, TSA, AIRLINES, HARTEVELDT\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BAN, COURTS, JUSTICE DEPARTMENT, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, WORKERS, UNIONS, SHULER, JOHN DEERE\par \par \uc2 CAPITOL, HOUSE, INVESTIGATION, BANNON, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 SUPPLIES, PORT, HOLIDAYS, SEROKA, MONKMEYER\par \par \uc2 DODGERS, GIANTS, PLAYOFFS, MLB\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 MURDAUGH, INSURANCE, SHOOTING, SMITH, HARPOOTLIAN\par \par \uc2 ADELE, "EASY ON ME", VIDEO, LIPSHITZ, DIVORCE\par \par \uc2 CLINTON, HOSPITALIZED, INFECTION, ALI\par \par \uc2 BOOSTER, VACCINE, MODERNA, \uc2 JOHNSON & JOHNSON\par \par \uc2 MURDER, TEXAS, BRYAN, GRISHAM, MURPHY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 "SQUID GAME", DIRECTOR, NETFLIX, DONG-HYUK\par \par \uc2 CASH, BUDGET, DEBT, FREE, GOAL, ANDERSON, ALICHE\par \par \uc2 JAMES, HIP HOP, FEMALE, RAPPERS\par \par \uc2 DOUGHTY\par \par \uc2 GARCIA, RYAN, SWEATER, FALL\par \par \uc2 SIRHAN, PAROLE, KENNEDY\par \par \uc2 FALL, ORGANIZATION, ROBIN\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Speaking of the fall chill, let's talk about getting organized for fall. This is a great time of year to start putting your house in order. Our segment is sponsored by \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2 , and, you know, just simplifying your household from your pantry to your wallet to your home office, you can live a fuller life. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JEN ROBIN, PROFESSIONAL HOME ORGANIZER & WELLS FARGO SPOKESPERSON\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Autumn is here and it's time to get organized and simplify. Professional home organizer and spokesperson Jen Robin is teaming up with our sponsor \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  to guide us through it all.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JEN'S TIPS FOR A JOYFUL LIFE! ORGANIZATION, FUNCTION, AESTHETICS\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) She says adding organization, function and aesthetics can add more joy to your life.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PANTRY MAKEOVER, DECANTING IS KEY\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) First up, a pantry makeover where decanting is the key to success.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SIMPLIFY YOUR HOUSEHOLD, TIPS FOR YOUR PANTRY, WALLET AND HOME OFFICE\par \par \uc2 JEN ROBIN (PROF HOME ORGANIZER)\par \par \uc2 This is a typical pantry for most people that we can really take this to the next level. Taking items out of their original packages and putting it into a container like this where you can see everything that you have. It keeps your food fresher longer.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PANTRY MAKEOVER, CREATE ZONES\par \par \uc2 JEN ROBIN (PROF HOME ORGANIZER)\par \par \uc2 It's so important to have everything in one place by zones. This is the pantry snack portion. You can buy any of these products online or at your local stores where they sell organizing products.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And the one personal item that needs a weekly edit, our wallets.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SIMPLIFY YOUR LIFE, IT ALL STARTS WITH A "WALLET EDIT"\par \par \uc2 JEN ROBIN (PROF HOME ORGANIZER)\par \par \uc2 Most people have way too much stuff in their wallet. It's always overflowing. What I like to do is what we call a wallet edit.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CREATE PILES, SAVE ESSENTIALS, TOSS NO NEEDS, FILE SET ASIDE\par \par \uc2 JEN ROBIN (PROF HOME ORGANIZER)\par \par \uc2 We're gonna be making piles such as save which is things that you need. Then toss, a pile for things that you do not need. And then file, things that you necessarily don't need in your wallet but you definitely should be keeping somewhere in your home. For me, the only card I need in my wallet is the \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  active cash \uc2 Visa\uc2  card. This card earns me unlimited two percent cash rewards on purchases, with no categories or rules to remember. This is what most people's wallets look like, and this is what your wallet can look like after a simple edit.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RESTYLE HOME OFFICE, MAKE SPACE FOR HOME OFFICE\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And finally, creating a home office that's both family friendly and efficient.\par \par \uc2 JEN ROBIN (PROF HOME ORGANIZER)\par \par \uc2 Many people don't have a designated home office. So what I've done is I've turned my breakfast nook into my home office. I created this cart specifically for my office items. That way I can easily transition from pulling something out to the kitchen nook to then helping the kids with their schoolwork and then transition one more time to dinner. I can easily put everything back and roll it back to the wall.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RESTYLE HOME OFFICE, USE LABELED CONTAINERS\par \par \uc2 JEN ROBIN (PROF HOME ORGANIZER)\par \par \uc2 It's super important to have structure in these spaces. We have containers that are labeled, paper, notebooks, any of your tech equipment. Easy to clean up, easy to maintain and everyone's happy.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I got a lot to do in organizing my household.\par \par \uc2 ROB MARCIANO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right. Coming up, Broadway is back. The cast of "Girl From the North Country" performs live in Times Square. Stay right there.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 "GMA's" "Simplify Your Life" is sponsored by....\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211029ehaf0003j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 JOHN DEERE WORKERS ON STRIKE 10,000 EMPLOYEES DEMANDING BETTER PAY\b0\par\par\uc2 516 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT CLINTON HOSPITALIZED\par \par \uc2 CLINTON, HOSPITAL, ICU, SEPSIS, ALI\par \par \uc2 VACCINES, BOOSTERS, FDA, BROWNSTEIN, MANDATES\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, VACCINE, TSA, AIRLINES, HARTEVELDT\par \par \uc2 ABORTION, BAN, COURTS, JUSTICE DEPARTMENT, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, WORKERS, UNIONS, SHULER, JOHN DEERE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, George. Now to America on strike. Some 60,000 film and television production workers threatening to walk off the job on Monday, while more than 10,000 John Deere workers are already walking the picket line. Terry Moran is live outside John Deere headquarters in Illinois for us this morning. Good morning, Terry.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN DEERE WORKERS ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 10,000 EMPLOYEES DEMANDING BETTER PAY AS COMPANY EXPECTS RECORD PROFITS\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good morning, Michael. They've been here all night all through this chilly night, the men and women on this picket line here at a John Deere facility across three states demanding what they say is their fair share from a hugely profitable American company. And they're not alone. Now as more workers across the country are hitting the picket lines, the question now, is Hollywood next?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, the more than 10,000 John Deere workers on strike stand united as they demand higher wages and better benefits. The United Autoworkers Union had vowed members would walk out if they could not settle on a contract. And now the pressure is on John Deere as the company that builds those iconic green tractors see its first major strike in three and a half decades.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Sounds like it's about sticking together in some ways.\par \par \uc2 WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 It's about sticking together now and for the people that come after us.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Workers rejected a proposal of just over $30 an hour for a top production worker, rising to 31.84 after five years. Bargaining for wages they say they deserve.\par \par \uc2 WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 They want to keep all the money in their pocket for their shareholders. The CEO continues to get massive raises and it doesn't add up when it comes to what they offered to us.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The workers' demands come as John Deere expects record profits of $5.9 billion this year.\par \par \uc2 LIZ SHULER (PRESIDENT AFL-CIO)\par \par \uc2 We are seeing workers standing up and exercising their power collectively to fight back against corporations who aren't sharing that wealth that workers have helped create.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLLYWOOD WALKOUT?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 60,000 FILM & TV WORKERS THREATEN STRIKE ON MONDAY\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And unless concessions are made in Hollywood there could be more economic fallout. The union representing 60,000 film and TV workers, from costume designers to audio technicians, warn its members are prepared to strike on Monday.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If that strike goes forward it could halt film and television production across the country, another sign of the rising spirit of activism and solidarity in the American labor movement. George.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211029ehaf0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 JOHN DEERE WORKERS GO ON STRIKE 10,000 EMPLOYEES WALK OFF THE JOB AMID CONTRACT DISPUTE\b0\par\par\uc2 563 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DEERE, PROTEST, HAGEN, BAHNER, KELLOGG'S, SHIPLEY\par \par \uc2 SUPPLY, SHORTAGE, BIDEN, ASHLEY, AYALA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TAKES ON SUPPLY CHAIN CRISIS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ANNOUNCES NEW STRATEGY TO SOLVE SHIPPING LOGJAM\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATION, CAPITOL, RIOT, TRUMP\par \par \uc2 SENIORS, SOCIAL SECURITY, BENEFITS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIG BUMP IN SOCIAL SECURITY BENEFITS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE MONEY FOR 70 MILLION SENIORS\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, BOOSTERS, FAUCI, ZIENTS, IRVING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FDA PANEL TO MEET OVER MODERNA & J&J BOOSTERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: KEY STEP IN AUTHORIZING ADDITIONAL DOSES FOR MILLIONS\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, JHA, BOOSTERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE BOOSTERS ON THE WAY?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WHAT TO EXPECT FROM FDA MEETINGS ON MODERNA & J&J VACCINE\par \par \uc2 NORWAY, BOW AND ARROW, ATTACK, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY BOW AND ARROW ATTACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUSPECT ACCUSED OF KILLING FIVE PEOPLE\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATION, US GYMNASTICS, BILES, RAISMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TOP GYMNASTS CALL FOR OUSTER OF U.S. OLYMPIC BOARD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ASKING CONGRESS TO INTERVENE IN WAKE OF LARRY NASSAR SCANDAL\par \par \uc2 WNBA, FINALS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 BLUE ORIGIN, SHATNER, SPACE, TRAVEL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILLIAM SHATNER'S OUT-OF-THIS-WORLD ADVENTURE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACTOR SPEAKS OUT AFTER HISTORIC SPACE LAUNCH\par \par \uc2 INVESTIGATION, MURDAUGH, SMITH\par \par \uc2 SAFETY, FRAUD, \uc2 GOOGLE\uc2  VOICE, FLEMING, LEWIS\par \par \uc2 SOCCER, WORLD CUP\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 JOHN DEERE, PROTEST, HOLLYWOOD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN DEERE WORKERS GO ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 10,000 EMPLOYEES WALK OFF THE JOB AMID CONTRACT DISPUTE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We have a lot of news this morning as well, starting with that breaking news overnight. John Deere workers have gone on strike. And this as some other possible strikes could impact health care and Hollywood. I wanna go back to Terry Moran. Good morning, Terry.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) George, this morning, that major news strike, 10,000 workers at John Deere walking off the jobs and on to the picket lines.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) They are looking for better pay and to keep their pensions. And they notice that the company is making record profit, $6 billion this year, shattering previous records. And the CEO of the company, he gave himself a 160% pay raise. They want more. Just the latest in a wave of labor stoppages across the country.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMERICA ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN DEERE WORKERS LATEST TO WALK OFF THE JOB\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nurses in California and Oregon at \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2 , they've authorized a strike. Workers at Kellogg's have been out on strike for about a week now.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLLYWQOD WALKOUT?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 60,000 FILM & TV CREW MEMBERS COULD SOON GO ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And that big one looming ahead, 60,000 film and TV crew members in Hollywood threatening to bring that whole industry to a halt.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) They are talking about unfair working conditions, and they've authorized a strike for Monday. Several forces driving all this. As the pandemic eases, companies are seeing sky-high demand and very tight labor markets. They can't fill jobs. Workers are noticing top executives and bosses are getting paid. They want more. They're willing to strike. And so, for the very first time in many, many years in our country, it looks like workers have the upper hand. Robin?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That's what we're seeing. Terry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211029ehae0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 JOHN DEERE WORKERS GO ON STRIKE) 10,000 EMPLOYEES WALK OFF THE JOB AMID CONTRACT DISPUTE\b0\par\par\uc2 559 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 JOHN DEERE, PROTEST, HAGEN, BAHNER, KELLOGG'S, SHIPLEY\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But we begin with that breaking news overnight, 10,000 John Deere workers going on strike. You're looking live at some employees hitting the picket lines in Illinois this morning.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN DEERE WORKERS GO ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 10,000 EMPLOYEES WALK OFF THE JOB AMID CONTRACT DISPUTE\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) This as some other strikes could impact health care and Hollywood. Terry Moran is tracking the very latest for us. Good morning, Terry.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good morning, Michael. That strike at John Deere just the latest in a wave of labor stoppages across the country. There is a new militant spirit in the American workforce. Workers want more and they're willing to strike to get it.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This morning, 10,000 workers at John Deere hit the picket lines in Iowa, Illinois and Kansas. They argue that even as the agricultural equipment company's profits grew by 61% in recent years and their CEO's salary grew by 160% during the pandemic, their pay has actually been cut.\par \par \uc2 LAURA HAGEN (JOHN DEERE EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 We need to let them know that they can't continue to take our money and take our money and take our money.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN DEERE WORKERS LATEST TO WALK OFF THE JOB\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Across the country, labor is on the march. In California and Oregon, 24,000 nurses and other health care workers at \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  voted to authorize a strike over better pay and better working conditions. Meanwhile, union workers at Kellogg's cereal plants have been on strike for more than a week, angry about long hours including seven-day workweeks.\par \par \uc2 KRIS BAHNER\uc2  (SPOKESPERSON KELLOGG'S)\par \par \uc2 Our number one priority is to get back in the negotiation table and reach a contract, so our employees can get back to their jobs and their lives.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLLYWOOD WALKOUT?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 60,000 FILMS & TV CREW MEMBERS COULD SOON GO ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And soon, Hollywood may see a walkout. 60,000 film and TV crew members could walk off the job Monday over what they claim are unfair working conditions.\par \par \uc2 MARISA SHIPLEY (IATSE LOCAL 871)\par \par \uc2 Our hours are really grueling and we need not just rest between the workdays and over the weekend, but an actual meal break during the day.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD NUMBER OF U.S. WORKERS QUITTING THEIR JOBS\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And across the country, a record number of workers are quitting their jobs. 4.3 million Americans or 2.9% of the workforce left their jobs in August. Many are looking for better work conditions and pay, especially with inflation on the rise.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Several forces are driving all of this. As the pandemic comes to an end, companies are seeing sky-high demand and very tight labor markets, they're having trouble filling jobs. Workers notice that top executives and bosses are getting paid. And so, for the first time in a long, long time, workers have the upper hand. Robin?\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) They've had enough. All right, Terry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211029ehae00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 FIGHTING BACK UNION WORKERS ACROSS THE COUNTRY STRIKE FOR FAIR PAY AND BENEFITS\b0\par\par\uc2 2,093 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Nightline\par \uc2 NLNE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 (c) Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NIGHTLINE\par \par \uc2 STRIKE, NAVA, SAUNDERS, HAGAN, WRIGHT, SPENCER, RHINE\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good evening. Thank you for joining us. Across the country, workers are fed up and striking back, hitting the picket lines, demanding better pay and benefits. Here's ABC's senior national correspondent, Terry Moran.\par \par \uc2 JANET NAVA (JOHN DEERE EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 I'm looking for what's fair to us.\par \par \uc2 JOSH SAUNDERS (JOHN DEERE EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 If we're critically essential workers because we make agricultural equipment and we feed the world, well, do we not deserve a critically essential wage and benefit?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the wee hours Thursday morning, 10,000 workers at John Deere hit the picket lines in Iowa, Illinois and Kansas, on strike for the first time in 35 years.\par \par \uc2 LAURA HAGEN (JOHN DEERE EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 They can't continue to take our money and take our money and take our money.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) They're demanding better pay, secure pensions, a fair share of a hugely profitable American company. The workers at John Deere voted overwhelmingly to reject the contract that their own union, the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , had negotiated with management.\par \par \uc2 TEIM NIEDERT (JOHN DEERE EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 There was a pretty blatant attack on our benefits. They want to us pay more for our benefits.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORKERS REJECTED 5% RAISE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMPANY ESTIMATES $5.9 BILLION 2021 PROFITS\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The deal offered a 5% raise, not what the workers expected from a company that estimates it will make $5.9 billion in profits in 2021.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN DEERE LOGO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN DEERE PROFITS UP 61%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CEO'S SALARY UP 160%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORKERS' PAY WAS CUT\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Workers argue that even as John Deere's profits grew by 61% in recent years and their CEO's salary grew by 160% during the pandemic, their pay has actually been cut.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, you want a bigger piece of a bigger pie?\par \par \uc2 JOSH SAUNDERS (JOHN DEERE EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, a bigger piece of the bigger pie.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We have about 700 billionaires in the US, and their total net worth $4.7 trillion. That is actually more than the bottom half of all Americans combined.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Something is happening in the American workforce. Employees want more, and they are willing to strike or even quit to get it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 4.3 MILLION AMERICANS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2.9% OF WORKFORCE LEFT JOBS\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A record-breaking 4.3 million Americans, or 2.9% of the workforce, left their jobs in August.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) American workers are tired. They are frustrated. They are burned out. And after 19 months through the pandemic, they're wondering what comes next for them.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) While John Deere employees are on day one of their fight, in Brookwood, Alabama, striking coal miners rally every Wednesday...\par \par \uc2 STRIKING COAL MINER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Every union in this country owes something to coal miners.\par \par \uc2 STRIKING COAL MINERS (GROUP)\par \par \uc2 Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Bolstered by their motto, "one day longer." But those days have turned into months. Seven months, now. Braxton Wright joined the picket lines after failing to reach a deal with mine owner, \uc2 Warrior Met Coal\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BRAXTON WRIGHT (WARRIOR MET COAL EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 Well, we knew it was gonna be a struggle and a fight.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Did you think in October, you'd still be here?\par \par \uc2 LARRY SPENCER (COAL MINER'S UNION VICE PRESIDENT)\par \par \uc2 I did think we would get something settled before now. And it's, it just seems to be getting worse.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Larry Spencer is the union vice president involved in the negotiations.\par \par \uc2 LARRY SPENCER (COAL MINER'S UNION VICE PRESIDENT)\par \par \uc2 I would like to say this part. They, they might think they're gonna break these people's spirit, but they're not.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) This is the first coal miner strike the town has seen in four decades. The miners in Brookwood are demanding an increase in wages and benefits.\par \par \uc2 BRAXTON WRIGHT (WARRIOR MET COAL EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 I believe the labor movement in the United States is, is growing. But I think a lot of them are watching us, because they know if we lose this fight, they're next.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATEMENT FROM WARRIOR MET COAL\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) ABC News reached out to \uc2 Warrior Met Coal\uc2  for a comment, but the company turned down our request for an interview due to the ongoing negotiations, telling ABC News, in part, "We remain steadfast in our belief that the eight proposals given to the union are fair and equitable. \uc2 Warrior Met Coal\uc2  provides some of the highest-paying jobs in Alabama. \uc2 Warrior Met Coal\uc2  continues to negotiate in good faith with the UMWA, and we remain hopeful for a fair and reasonable resolution."\par \par \uc2 LARRY SPENCER (COAL MINER'S UNION VICE PRESIDENT)\par \par \uc2 We fight hard every day and try to make sure these workers get treated fairly, and I plan on continuing to fight just as long as I can.\par \par \uc2 DAN OSBORN (PRESIDENTOMAHA CHAPTER BCTGM UNION)\par \par \uc2 Kind of a David-and-Goliath fight right now.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Last week, 1,400 Kellogg's Cereal workers launched strikes in Tennessee, Nebraska, Michigan and Pennsylvania, angry about what they say are long hours, including seven day work weeks.\par \par \uc2 DAN OSBORN (PRESIDENTOMAHA CHAPTER BCTGM UNION)\par \par \uc2 This is a simple matter of fact, that the, the rich want to get richer. And, and to do that, they take away from the small guys.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN (LT.GOVERNOR OF PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 If you appreciate having your favorite cereal or your favorite product on a shelf, you know, you need to make sure that the people that made that possible are able to live a good, stable, dignified existence.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) John Fetterman is the lieutenant governor of Pennsylvania and a fierce supporter of unions. Fetterman is currently fundraising for Kellogg's workers picketing on the front lines.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN (LT.GOVERNOR OF PENNSYLVANIA)\par \par \uc2 These are the men and women that fed us during a pandemic, and they stepped up throughout the entire time. And they didn't have the luxury of standing down.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) A Kellogg's spokesperson says the company has been, quote, "grossly misrepresented by the unions, and that Kellogg's top priority is reaching a contract."\par \par \uc2 KRIS BAHNER\uc2  (KELLOGG COMPANY SPOKESPERSON)\par \par \uc2 We value all our employees and recognize their efforts, especially during this global pandemic. We're especially concerned that the union struck without allowing members to vote on the company's October 1st offer.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In California and Oregon, 24,000 nurses and other health care workers at \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  voted to authorize a strike over pay and working conditions. And soon, Hollywood may see a walkout. Roughly 60,000 film and TV workers are getting their picket signs ready, set to go on strike on Monday.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA RHINE (NATIONAL EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR IATSE)\par \par \uc2 This is the moment. This is the fight.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If it happens, it would be the first nationwide strike in that powerful union's history and could bring Hollywood to a halt.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA RHINE (NATIONAL EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR IATSE)\par \par \uc2 We have some members in our crafts that are so underpaid that they can't possibly survive in the cities where they provide services.\par \par \uc2 MARISA SHIPLEY (IATSE LOCAL 871)\par \par \uc2 Our hours are really grueling, and we need not just rest between the workdays and over the weekend, but an actual meal break during the day.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But on the growing number of picket lines and across America, it's about more than money. It's about respect.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Sounds like it's about sticking together (inaudible) ?\par \par \uc2 JOSH SAUNDERS (JOHN DEERE EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, it's about sticking together now, and for the people that come after us.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATEMENT FROM \uc2 JOHN DEERE COMPANY\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) John Deere releasing a statement to ABC News, reading, in part, "John Deere is committed to a favorable outcome for everyone involved and is committed to reach an agreement with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  that would put every employee in a better economic position and continue to make them the highest-paid employees in the agriculture and construction industries. Our immediate concern is meeting the needs of our customers." The workers who make those iconic green tractors are bracing for the long haul.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) How long are you prepared to do this for?\par \par \uc2 JOSH SAUNDERS (JOHN DEERE EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 As long as we have to. Whatever that takes, however long that takes to do, we're ready.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Our thanks to Terry. Earlier this evening, I spoke to Andrew Johnson, a Kellogg's assembly line worker on strike in Lancaster, Pennsylvania. Andrew, thank you so much for joining us. As we saw in the piece, Kellogg's workers are striking across the country. Tell me why you're on strike.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW JOHNSON (KELLOGG'S EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 I'm on strike for my family, my children. They deserve, when they get to the workforce, to be able to earn a fair wage, have good health care, and be able to retire someday. And the way corporate America's going, it's not going to be there for them unless us, the working class, stand up and fight for them.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Talk to me about being an essential worker during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW JOHNSON (KELLOGG'S EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 It was a lot of long hours, hard days. When you go in there at the beginning, and they told us we were essential workers, there was a sense of pride behind that, that America needs us. And it felt good, because we were taking care of America, only a year later to be told we were nothing and the company needed us to give them more concessions.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Kellogg's is a, such a well-known American brand. What is it you want consumers to know about the company?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW JOHNSON (KELLOGG'S EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 That they need to stop being so greedy. Kellogg's was a fabulous company to work for. They always took care of their workers. But today, all they want to do is line the CEO's pockets with a bigger bonus every year, and they don't care about the employees.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) If you had the opportunity to send a message to executives, what will you say to them?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW JOHNSON (KELLOGG'S EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 We go to work every day to make the company the billions and billions of dollars in profits that they make. Without us, they won't make that money. Take care of us, and we'll take care of you.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Andrew, you mentioned earlier that you're a father. How old are your kids?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW JOHNSON (KELLOGG'S EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 My oldest is 10, middle one is six, and my youngest is two-and-a-half.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) How do you explain this to them?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW JOHNSON (KELLOGG'S EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 I tell them that this is something that has to happen for their future, so that they're able to have a good job, someday. I have to go to work to provide the life that they deserve, and I hope when they get older, that they understand the long hours that I was away wasn't about me, it was about them.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) How long can you hold out?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW JOHNSON (KELLOGG'S EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 I'll be on that street for a year before I'm willing to concede to anything. The union, probably longer.\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Lines have been drawn. Andrew Johnson, thank you so much for your time. We wish the best to you and your family and to your union brothers and sisters.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW JOHNSON (KELLOGG'S EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Byron.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEXT ON\par \par \uc2 BYRON PITTS\uc2  (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And up next, Matt Damon and Ben Affleck, the longtime friends back on the big screen in "The Last Duel."\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP: "THE LAST DUEL"\par \par \uc2 ACTOR (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Let them go. Let them go.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document NLNE000020211015ehae00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THOUSANDS ON STRIKE JOHN DEERE EMPLOYEES DEMAND BETTER PAY, A "DECENT LIVING"\b0\par\par\uc2 583 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, HAYES, OFFIT, MODERNA, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 JHA, COVID-19, VACCINE, BOOSTERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID BOOSTER SHOTS\par \par \uc2 MAJORS, SENTENCING, LEWIS\par \par \uc2 PROTESTS, JOBS, WAGE INCREASE, NIEDERT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS ON STRIKE\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now to American workers. And tonight, the major strike underway, 10,000 John Deere workers on the picket line arguing that, while company profits and CEO pay have soared, their pay increases have not kept up. Thousands of workers also striking at Kellogg, Kaiser Hospitals, and Hollywood now bracing for a potential walkout, too. ABC's senior national correspondent, Terry Moran, in Illinois, tonight.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, more than 10,000 workers at John Deere are on the picket lines in Iowa, Illinois and Kansas, on strike for the first time in 35 years at Deere.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOHN DEERE EMPLOYEES DEMAND BETTER PAY, A "DECENT LIVING"\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) They're demanding better pay, secure pensions, what they call a fair share of a hugely profitable American company.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What's at stake, here? Why are you guys out here?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DEERE WORKER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 The raise that was offered to us wasn't what we were expecting. And the company wants to eliminate pensions for the people that hire after us.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Workers argue that what John Deere is offering them now is nowhere near enough, as John Deere's profits grew by 61% in recent years and their CEO's salary grew by 160% during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DEERE WORKER (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 We work and work, and it's time. It's to get what we deserve.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) After weeks of negotiations, the workers at John Deere voted overwhelmingly to reject a contract that their own union, the United Autoworkers, had negotiated with management.\par \par \uc2 TIM NIEDERT (JOHN DEERE WORKER)\par \par \uc2 There was a pretty blatant attack on our benefits.\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The union is standing behind its decision to turn down the deal, which offered a 5% raise in the first year, not what the workers expected at a company that estimates it will make $5.9 billion in profits in 2021.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORKERS NATIONWIDE PRESSING FOR BETTER PAY AND CONDITIONS\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nationwide, more and more workers are flexing their collective muscles. Last week, 1,400 Kellogg's cereal workers launched strikes in Tennessee, Nebraska, Michigan and Pennsylvania. In California and Oregon, 24,000 nurses and other health care workers at \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  voted to authorize a strike over pay and better working conditions. And soon, Hollywood may see a walkout. Roughly 60,000 film and TV workers are set to go on strike on Monday. Tonight, the women and men here on the picket line, the people who build those iconic green John Deere farm vehicles, they're bracing for a long strike.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And Terry's live outside John Deere headquarters, tonight. Where do negotiations stand, Terry?\par \par \uc2 TERRY MORAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, David, John Deere in a statement says it's committed to reaching a favorable outcome for everyone, but there's no word when negotiations might resume. Meanwhile, on these picket lines, the emotions are so intense, it feels like, after years of frustration, a dam is bursting, here and around the country. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Terry Moran, from Illinois, tonight. Terry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020211015ehae00005\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SWITCHING CAREERS IN YOUR 50'S OLDER ADULTS ACHIEVING PROFESSIONAL FULFILLMENT\b0\par\par\uc2 823 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SUPPLY CHAIN SHORTAGES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: US TO REOPEN BORDERS\par \par \uc2 SUPPLY CHAIN, SHORTAGES, BIDEN, PORTS\par \par \uc2 LOGJAM, PORTS, ECONOMY, BORDERS, VACCINATED\par \par \uc2 VACCINE, MANDATES, NETS, IRVING, TEXAS, KIDS, FDA\par \par \uc2 SPIES, PLOT, SUBMARINES, TOEBBE, \uc2 FBI\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, ACRES, WINDS, TORNADOES, FREEZE\par \par \uc2 ASPIRIN, GUIDANCE, HEART ATTACK, STROKE\par \par \uc2 LIFTOFF, SHATNER, BLUE ORIGIN, SPACE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 FILM CLIP FROM "GOOD WILL HUNTING"\par \par \uc2 PETITO, LAUNDRIE, AUTOPSY, STRANGLED, BOYCE\par \par \uc2 NFL\uc2 , GRUDEN, RACIST, HOMOPHOBIC, EMAILS\par \par \uc2 PUMPKINS, SHORTAGES, JACKSON, CROPS, CHRISTMAS\par \par \uc2 GOLFER, GROUNDSKEEPER, TURF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 SHATNER, SPACE TRAVEL, BLUE ORIGIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COUNTDOWN TO LIFTOFF\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WILLIAM SHATNER & BLUE ORIGIN HOURS AWAY FROM LAUNCH\par \par \uc2 SALT, GUIDELINES, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 ECONOMY, JOBS, COVID\par \par \uc2 "DANCING WITH THE STARS", "MUPPETS HAUNTED MANSION"\par \par \uc2 SAVINGS, MUHAMMAD, DEBT\par \par \uc2 SAVINGS, DEBT, RICHARDSON\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: OVERCOMING DEBT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIPS TO HELP GET YOUR FINANCIAL HOUSE IN ORDER\par \par \uc2 KENT, AGURCIA, CAIN, NORTH CAROLINA, BILTMORE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RISE & SHINE\par \par \uc2 AFFLECK, DAMON, "THE LAST DUEL"\par \par \uc2 BROWN, PAGLIANO, HAMPEL, WEDDING\par \par \uc2 ADULTS, CAREERS, KARBOWSKI, WEINSTOCK\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Turning now to building new career skills. Older adults are increasingly looking for professional fulfillment later in life, whether it's switching jobs or growing their own careers. This segment is sponsored by... committed to providing tools to embrace exciting opportunities. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Fifty-nine-year-old Kendra Karbowski is the matriarch of her blended family from Omaha, Nebraska. She spent most of her career as a community health educator.\par \par \uc2 KENDRA KARBOWSKI (STAND-UP COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I felt I really had a purpose to spread education and information.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But there was always something else she dreamed of doing.\par \par \uc2 KENDRA KARBOWSKI (STAND-UP COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 I grew up wanting to be Carol Burnett.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And at the age of 52 she left her job at the Health Department to pursue stand-up comedy.\par \par \uc2 KENDRA KARBOWSKI (STAND-UP COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 Too often we get our first husbands like our first house 'cause it's cute. That second one you get it inspected.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Like many, she says changing careers in her 50's was definitely scary.\par \par \uc2 KENDRA KARBOWSKI (STAND-UP COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 You know, it's a bit of a struggle when you're a female comedienne anyway. And then if you're over 50 there is some ageism there.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) But she has no regrets.\par \par \uc2 KENDRA KARBOWSKI (STAND-UP COMEDIAN)\par \par \uc2 You're making people feel good and it's just a dream come true to be able to do this.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 31 MILLION PEOPLE 50+ IN THE WORKFORCE\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And Kendra's not alone in the desire for professional fulfillment later in life. There are more than 31 million people in their 50s in the workforce today.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN WEINSTOCK (SPOKESPERSON & VP FINANCIAL RESILIENCE PGRM AARP)\par \par \uc2 The value that they bring to the workforce, that empathy, the collaboration, the teamwork, those soft skills are something that you get from a lifetime of work. And those are things that older workers have in spades.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Susan Weinstock is a spokesperson for our sponsor AARP and has some tips for older adults, no matter what they're looking for in a career.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CAREER CHANGE ADVICE #1, USE SOCIAL NETWORKING\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) First, use social media to expand your professional network.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN WEINSTOCK (SPOKESPERSON & VP FINANCIAL RESILIENCE PGRM AARP)\par \par \uc2 Look at people's profiles. See if you know someone who knows someone and ask for an introduction. And maybe talk to them a little bit about the industry that they work in or the employer that they work for.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CAREER CHANGE ADVICE #2, TRY A SIDE HUSTLE\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Next, do a trial run.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN WEINSTOCK (SPOKESPERSON & VP FINANCIAL RESILIENCE PGRM AARP)\par \par \uc2 If this is a dream you've had for years, then do a side hustle. It's a great way to sort of get to know it. Or try volunteering and then decide this, I'm gonna try and go for this full time.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CAREER CHANGE ADVICE #3, BOOST YOUR SKILLS\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And make sure your skills are up to date.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN WEINSTOCK (SPOKESPERSON & VP FINANCIAL RESILIENCE PGRM AARP)\par \par \uc2 AARP just launched our skills builder for work which is an online learning platform. We're offering free courses in personal creativity, in remote work and in using computer tools for an office environment. And people can get certifications and take a whole myriad of courses.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The idea is to grow skills to help you take charge of your career with confidence.\par \par \uc2 LARA SPENCER (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yep, great tips. And coming up, we meet the creative team of the new play "Thoughts of a Colored Man" making history on Broadway.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 "GMA" Better With Age is sponsored by...\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211027ehad0003k\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 AMAZON EXTENDS WORK-FROM-HOME POLICY MORE EMPLOYERS OFFERING FLEXIBILITY TO RETAIN EMPLOYEES\b0\par\par\uc2 720 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 WILDFIRE, EVACUATIONS, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 PLANE, CRASH, NELSON, KEELEY, RAY, PELLOTH\par \par \uc2 TRAVEL, VACCINES, \uc2 SOUTHWEST AIRLINES\uc2 , JONES, MURRAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTHWEST CANCELLATIONS CHAOS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PASSENGERS LEFT STRANDED AFTER AIRLINE SCRAPS THOUSANDS OF FLIGHTS\par \par \uc2 CANCELLATIONS, KELLY, \uc2 SOUTHWEST AIRLINES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTHWEST CEO ON CANCELLATIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PASSENGERS LEFT STRANDED AFTER AIRLINE SCRAPS THOUSANDS OF FLIGHTS\par \par \uc2 TOEBBE, INVESTIGATION, BANNAT, SUBMARINE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ACCUSED SUBMARINE SPIES DUE IN COURT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NAVY ENGINEER & WIFE CHARGED WITH TRYING TO SELL U.S. MILITARY SECRETS\par \par \uc2 RAMIREZ, SAFETY, NUNEZ, HAFLIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEARTWARMING HOMECOMING FOR TEXAS 3-YEAR-OLD\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RELEASED FROM HOSPITAL AFTER MIRACULOUS RESCUE\par \par \uc2 BOSTON, BASEBALL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 RED SOX\uc2  CLINCH DIVISION SERIES WITH WALKOFF WIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BOSTON BEATS TAMPA BAY TO ADVANCE TO ALCS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RIGHT NOW\par \par \uc2 DISCRIMINATION, GRUDEN, RESIGNATION, ROSMAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JON GRUDEN OUT AS RAIDERS COACH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RESIGNS AMID SCANDAL OVER PAST RACIST & HOMOPHOBIC EMAILS\par \par \uc2 MARROW, RESCUE, MANHOLE, ACCIDENT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HERO MOM SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN WHO JUMPED INTO OPEN MANHOLE TO SAVE TODDLER TELLS HER STORY\par \par \uc2 QR CODES, SCAM, OSTROWSKI, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW WARNING ABOUT QR CODES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW SCAMMERS MAY BE CREATING FAKE ONES TO STEAL YOUR MONEY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRE, EVACUATIONS, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, FDA, BOOSTERS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE BOOSTERS ON THE WAY?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FDA PANEL TO MEET ON ADDITIONAL DOSES OF MODERNA & J&J VACCINES\par \par \uc2 AMAZON, WORK, HOME\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMAZON EXTENDS WORK-FROM-HOME POLICY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MORE EMPLOYERS OFFERING FLEXIBILITY TO RETAIN EMPLOYEES\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now to \uc2 Amazon\uc2  announcing a big change in its work from home policy for tech and corporate workers. This change could have a ripple effect in other corporations and industries. Rebecca Jarvis is back with the story. Hey, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Hey, Michael. Nice to see you. And this is a very significant change, both for employers overall as well as for \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , they are now saying you can work remotely indefinitely as long as you're able to commute to the office on occasion and your team says it's okay.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In a note to employees, CEO of Amazon writing that he's going to be leaving this up to the individual teams. The director/manager on each team will have to make the call and say whether it works for their group. And this is a big change from what \uc2 Amazon\uc2  was saying just a few weeks ago when they were telling employees they would need to return to the office by January for three days a week. It's also a big competitive move on their part. There were so many tech companies and peers that have made flexible work more a part of the culture and the ethos.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And with so many people now voluntarily leaving jobs at near record numbers and a record number, millions of them, of job openings, this makes \uc2 Amazon\uc2  more competitive with peers like Apple and \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , which have also adjusted their policies recently. But there is a ripple effect to these changes. You have the other types of businesses that surround these big corporations, the restaurants, the cafes, the gyms, those get less foot traffic when you have businesses telling their employees they don't have to return to the office every day. So, guys, there's that ripple effect on the local business ecosystem and there's also the ripple effect on the competition who says, you know what, we might need more flexible work too. Guys?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It's a very big move.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It is.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thank you, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GENDER-FREE LEGOS?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Coming up, Lego is letting go of gender stereotypes. What prompted the company to change?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE SCIENCE OF YOU\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And the science of you. Using glucose monitors and apps even if you don't have diabetes. How the information might help you with nutrition and more.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: EASY WEEKNIGHT MEALS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And \uc2 Instagram\uc2  favorite Carla Lalli Music shows us how to make 15-minute weeknight meals. She's with us live. We'll be right back.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211027ehac0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 ENGINE CATCHES FIRE HOLLYWOOD STRIKE LOOMING\b0\par\par\uc2 264 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Sunday\par \uc2 WNSU\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR OIL SPILL\par \par \uc2 OIL SPILL, PACIFIC, BEACHES, ECOLOGICAL, WILLSHER, ORE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MAJOR OIL SPILL\par \par \uc2 VACCINE, MANDATE, NYC, COVID, DEATHS, FAUCI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VACCINE MANDATE DEADLINE\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, LEGISLATION, DEMOCRATS, IMPASSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN'S AGENDA STALLED\par \par \uc2 MIGRANTS, SURGE, BORDER, COVID, ASYLUM, GELERNT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MIGRANT SURGE WARNINGS\par \par \uc2 BOMBING, MOSQUE, DEADLY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEADLY MOSQUE BOMBING\par \par \uc2 MARCH, PROTESTS, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT\par \par \uc2 MISSING, MARCANO, DISCOVERY, MINA, CABALLERO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEVASTATING DISCOVERY\par \par \uc2 SHIPPING, BACKLOG, SHORTAGES, LOVELY, TERRAZAS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RECORD SHIPPING BACKLOGS\par \par \uc2 PLANE, FIRE, HOLLYWOOD, STRIKE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ENGINE CATCHES FIRE\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Tonight, passengers forced to perform an emergency evacuation after an airplane engine burst into flames, struck by a bird.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Video shows passengers sliding down a \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2  plane's emergency exit chute, there, at Atlantic City International Airport. Officials say two people suffered minor injuries.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOLLYWOOD STRIKE LOOMING\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And 60,000 Hollywood workers, from camera operators to makeup artists, are gearing up for a walkout.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Members of the International Association of Theatrical Stage Employees are voting tonight on what would be the union's first nationwide strike. Stars like Ted Danson posting their support on social media. A strike would halt nearly all TV and film productions across the country.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) When we come back, harrowing footage of neighbors rescuing a 97-year-old woman whose house burst into flames.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And just how big has that Powerball prize grown? We'll tell you, coming up.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document WNSU000020211004eha30000a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BBB FINDS SURGE IN EMPLOYMENT SCAMS HOW SCAMMERS ARE TAKING ADVANTAGE OF JOB SEEKERS\b0\par\par\uc2 718 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, SURGE, HOTEZ, RAYNE, SAFETY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, VACCINES, PLAN, COVID-19\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIDEN'S NEW COVID BATTLE PLAN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT TO UNVEIL SIX-PRONGED STRATEGY TO STOP DELTA & BOOST VACCINATIONS\par \par \uc2 BAN, ABORTION, GARLAND, TEXAS\par \par \uc2 WEATHER, TRACK, TROPICAL STORM MINDY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TROPICAL STORM MAKES LANDFALL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: MINDY SLAMS INTO FLORIDA PANHANDLE\par \par \uc2 STORM, IDA, TRACK, WEATHER\par \par \uc2 SECURITY, CAPITOL HILL\par \par \uc2 TRIAL, PARIS, TERROR ATTACK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FRANCE'S "TRIAL OF THE CENTURY" BEGINS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 20 FACING CHARGES IN 2015 PARIS TERROR ATTACK\par \par \uc2 NEWSOM, RECALL ELECTION, HARRIS, OBAMA, ELDER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIVE DAYS TO HIGH-STAKES CALIFORNIA RECALL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: VP HARRIS & TOP DEMOCRATS RALLY TO SUPPORT GOV NEWSOM\par \par \uc2 HOLMES, THERANOS, TRIAL\par \par \uc2 SOCCER, PEPI\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RISE & SHINE\par \par \uc2 MURDAUGH, INVESTIGATION, LAWYER, LICENSE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOUTH CAROLINA MURDER MYSTERY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PROMINENT LAWYER'S LICENSE SUSPENDED AFTER ALLEGEDLY MISUSING FUNDS\par \par \uc2 RACISM, BROWN, LIGHTFOOT, SULTER, DOG\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMAN IN POLICE CONFRONTATION SPEAKS OUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CLAIMS RACIAL BIAS IN ALTERCATION WHILE WALKING DOG IN CHICAGO PARK\par \par \uc2 AIRFARE, TRAVEL, DAMODARAN, KIRBY, CAR, RENTAL\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AIRFARE ALERT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DOMESTIC FARES EXPECTED TO DROP 10 PERCENT IN SEPTEMBER\par \par \uc2 NEWLYWED, DIVING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PLAY OF THE DAY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID SURGE\par \par \uc2 CHILDREN, SCHOOL, COVID, CASES, HOTEZ, RAYNE\par \par \uc2 BBB, SCAMS, EMPLOYMENT\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now we're going to turn to a "GMA" exclusive and a surge in employment scams as people try to take advantage of those who lost their jobs during the pandemic. Trevor Ault joins us now with more on the scams and how you can spot them. Good morning, Trevor.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR AULT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good morning, Michael. These have been difficult economic times, a lot of people looking for work, and sadly this is one of the results. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  data shows that in 2020, Americans lost more than $62 million in employment scams. And the Better Business Bureau says frequently the target of these scams were younger people, two-thirds of them were under 45, and a lot of them said that they had recently been applying for a lot of jobs. Now, these scammers almost always found their marks online. In fact, they'd frequently go as far as conducting bogus online interviews where they would almost never show their face. And it's in these interviews a lot of the time when they were able to secure a person's personal or financial information. Things like asking for a person's bank account because they said that they needed it in order to get them their paycheck.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIPS FOR AVOIDING GETTING RIPPED OFF\par \par \uc2 TREVOR AULT (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now, the Better Business Bureau does have some tips that you can use in order to avoid falling prey to these scams. You want to make sure you're double-checking the job listing, go to the company's website, even call the company if you have to. You also want to double-check the email of the person that you're corresponding with, it should match exactly with the protocol of the company. It shouldn't be slightly different. It shouldn't be something generic like Gmail. You also want to make sure that you are never clicking on any links that are being sent from a phone number or an email that you don't recognize. And also, just in general, a good tip is that if you have to pay a person or a company in order to start working for them, it's probably a scam. Guys.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All great to keep in mind. Trevor, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA MORNING MENU\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMY KLOBUCHAR LIVE\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Coming up, our exclusive live interview with Senator Amy Klobuchar, speaking for the first time about an issue with her health. That's just ahead.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RISE & SHINE KANSAS\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Plus, we're live from the great state of Kansas this morning with some beautiful sunflowers and great food.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALS & STEALS\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And speaking of food, George, we are bringing home the bacon. It's one of the great "Deals & Steals" Tory Johnson has for us this morning. All of them made in America. That's all coming up on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020211004eh990000l\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 "TATTLEWARE" COMPANIES USE TECH TO TRACK REMOTE WORKERS\b0\par\par\uc2 397 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ON THE MOVE\par \par \uc2 HOLIDAY, TRAVEL, COVID, AIRPORTS, ROADS\par \par \uc2 JHA, CORONAVIRUS, TRAVEL, SURGE, BOOSTERS, MASKS\par \par \uc2 IDA, LOUISIANA, COMMUNITIES, FLOODING, POWER\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRES, HEAT, ACRES, WINDS\par \par \uc2 TALIBAN\uc2 , REBELS, STRONGHOLD, AMERICANS, STERN\par \par \uc2 BENEFITS, UNEMPLOYMENT, AID, ENDING\par \par \uc2 "TATTLEWARE", TECH, REMOTE, EMPLOYEES, COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) On the subject of work, check this out. There's a debate over working from home on accountability versus invasion of privacy.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "TATTLEWARE"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMPANIES USE TECH TO TRACK REMOTE WORKERS\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Employers are turning to so-called tattleware that could give them the ability to spy on remote workers to make sure their employees are actually working when they're working from home. One example is a digital platform, a digital surveillance platform called Sneak. It takes a live photo of workers every minute from their company laptops. Other programs track everything from your keystrokes to your web browsing. Guys, what do you think? Go.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well when you're checking your fantasy football team, Dan, we're all going to know it.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I invite the employers to take snapshots of me every minute. You'll see me napping with just a little bit of drool coming right down.\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) With Toby the cat.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) In the sweatpants.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TENNIS STARS UNDER PRESSURE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SLOANE STEPHENS REVEALS DISTURBING SOCIAL MEDIA MESSAGES\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) That'll be fun to see for everybody. All right, we do have a lot to cover this morning, and we're following a lot of other headlines as well, including that struggle off the court for tennis star Sloane Stephens, revealing that she received thousands of abusive messages after losing her match at the US Open. As Naomi Osaka said she's stepping away from the game as well.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: NEW TWIST IN SOUTH CAROLINA MURDER MYSTERY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FATHER SHOT MONTHS AFTER WIFE, SON WERE KILLED\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And a stunning new development in the murders of a mother and son from a prominent South Carolina family, now the father is recovering after being shot in broad daylight. But first, let's go back to Ginger and get a check of the weather.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210928eh9600030\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 DISAPPOINTING AUGUST JOB NUMBERS SHARP DROP FROM EARLIER SUMMER GAINS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,498 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: IDA'S DESTRUCTION\par \par \uc2 IDA, DEVASTATION, CLEANUP\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, HURRICANE, DAMAGE, LOUISIANA\par \par \uc2 COVID, UNVACCINATED, TRAVEL, HOSPITALS, SKOG\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, LAWSUITS\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, LAW, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 ARBERY, DA\par \par \uc2 TROPICS\par \par \uc2 OSAKA, DEFEAT, BREAK, TENNIS\par \par \uc2 JOBS, DECREASE\par \par \uc2 EVA PILGRIM (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We start this half hour, though, with the disappointing numbers from the latest jobs report, falling far short of estimates. President Biden blaming the lackluster figures on the pandemic. ABC's Deirdre Bolton is here with more on the challenge to fill government jobs on a number of levels. Good morning to you, Deirdre.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good morning, Eva. Towns, cities and states, they are competing seriously for workers. And, in fact, some are offering never-before-seen bonuses and other kinds of incentives.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BIGGEST HITS, RESTAURANTS, BARS, RETAIL, GOVERNMENT EMPLOYMENT DECLINING\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Hiring in the US downshifted abruptly in August with the smallest jobs gain in seven months. Restaurants and bars and retail took the biggest hits. Government employment also declined.\par \par \uc2 READE PICKERT (US ECONOMY REPORTER BLOOMBERG NEWS)\par \par \uc2 In conversations with local governments, for them this is the, the worst worker shortage that they've seen in their lifetimes.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) With more than 10 million jobs unfilled in the US economy, a record, there's more competition than ever for workers.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOVERNMENT WORK, 1 IN 10 JOBS ARE OPEN\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) In the broad category of government work, one in ten jobs are open.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SOLUTIONS ON A LOCAL LEVEL, TOWNS, CITIES AND STATES TAKING A PAGE FROM CORPORATE AMERICA\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) To fix the problem on a local level, towns, cities and states are taking a page from corporate America\par \par \uc2 READE PICKERT (US ECONOMY REPORTER BLOOMBERG NEWS)\par \par \uc2 In Tulsa, Oklahoma, they're offering $2,000 bonuses for 911 dispatchers.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Albuquerque is offering several incentives to bring in workers, including those from nearby cities or even states.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JOB VACANCIES, INCENTIVES TO ATTRACT WORKERS\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY ROMERO (HUMAN RESOURCES DIRECTOR CITY OF ALBUQUERQUE)\par \par \uc2 We have a bonus of $15,000 attached to lateral police officers who come from different public - safety agencies. Teachers and teaching assistants, they're being offered $750. In the 22 years that I've been with the city, this is the first time that we've seen something like this happen.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA ALBRIGHT (MUNICIPAL FINANCE REPORTER BLOOMBERG NEWS)\par \par \uc2 So they're having to do things that may be completely unfamiliar to them like signing bonuses. You don't necessarily think of a government as offering a signing bonus the way that say a Wall Street bank would.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BALTIMORE\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Baltimore is one of those cities struggling, facing a persistent shortage in its public works department where a quarter of the jobs are stubbornly available. Baltimore also upped the pay of city truck drivers to about $23 an hour, a raise of up to 5 percent, by postponing hiring in other departments. But it is still struggling to attract enough applicants.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Some city leaders telling us it is really difficult for them to compete with the private sector. One reason, most city jobs are in person and that leaves working remotely off the table. Dan.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Deirdre Bolton, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Let's bring in the US secretary of labor Marty Walsh who joins us exclusively this morning. Secretary, thanks for coming on. As you heard there, Deirdre's report talked about how many municipal jobs are going unfilled. How can that be turned around?\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2  (US SECRETARY OF LABOR)\par \par \uc2 Well, thank you for having me today. And certainly after yesterday's report we've been looking at, you know, a lot of the results. I think that some of the folks that are coming in into your report, you mentioned municipal employees or - potential workers not coming in because they wanna telework. I think we, we still have to focus on the delta variant and the coronavirus and we have to do everything we can to get people vaccinated and get those numbers down.\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2  (US SECRETARY OF LABOR)\par \par \uc2 We saw a spike in the month of August in, in the coronavirus which obviously was, was part and party to hurting our numbers being high, so we have some work to do there, there's no question about it. And I also think that school starting this week and next week is gonna help us as well. Lots of people having lots of problems with child care, particularly - single mothers and individual family members having a difficult time trying to have a place for their kids to go. So, again, with school open in person, as long as we continue to move forward here, we should be in a lot better shape as we move into the fall.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What about places where schools may not be able to reopen? Can you help parents with child care in order - to get them back into the labor force?\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2  (US SECRETARY OF LABOR)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, the President, yeah, absolutely. The President put forth in the American Rescue Plan $39 billion for the child care industry. Certainly that was restaurants, but child care industries were hit hard too during the pandemic because a lot of people stayed home and they didn't send their kids to childcare. So there is a $39 billion investment that right now is going, that has gone a couple of months ago to the states. And the states are, are allocating - those dollars to childcare facilities. We have to get them open.\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2  (US SECRETARY OF LABOR)\par \par \uc2 I mean, we're gonna be at a point, at some point in our - time here in the United States of we need to get more and more people back to work. The positive side is - I do wanna talk a little about the entire picture. Since the President has taken office, 12.5 million Americans have come to work. This month of August is one snapshot of a longer recovery.\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2  (US SECRETARY OF LABOR)\par \par \uc2 Obviously, we knew it was gonna be a long recovery. There's no roadmap on how to come back from a pandemic so we're talking about a, a glimpse of one month. But certainly, we all know, and the President knows and, and the administration knows, that we have work to do. There's no, no, no hiding that. And that's why the President's been so insistent on people being vaccinated. That is the way to make sure that you keep yourself safe and help, help us defeat this virus.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Speaking of the work that needs to be done, I wanna talk about another pain point for many people. The enhanced federal unemployment benefits that have been in place since last year, they're gonna end this weekend. What kind of impact will that have and do you favor extending these benefits?\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2  (US SECRETARY OF LABOR)\par \par \uc2 Well, what - what myself and Secretary Yellen did is we, we sent a, a letter to the states allowing them the opportunity to, to use the American Rescue Plan if they need to extend the unemployment benefit. Right now, when, when Congress passed that legislation, they put a time limit on it. September 6th was the timeline, and, and that's when the $300 ends.\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2  (US SECRETARY OF LABOR)\par \par \uc2 And hopefully, as we think about what's happening, we don't need it consistently across the country because we have some states with very low unemployment rates. It varies from state to state and, quite honestly, it varies from city to city. So, if a state needs to extend those unemployment benefits, they have the ability to through the American Rescue Plan dollars, which they have right now.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) US Secretary of Labor, Marty Walsh, thanks for coming on on a Saturday morning. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2  (US SECRETARY OF LABOR)\par \par \uc2 Thank you for having me today.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It's great to have somebody with a Boston accent on the show on a Saturday morning.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) There you go.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I almost called him Marty Walsh but didn't know if that would be appropriate.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) He would have appreciated it.\par \par \uc2 DAN HARRIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah, he probably would've.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right. Let's go back to the weather. Rob Marciano out there in Rye Brook, New York, there. The damage just incredible. People cleaning up, Rob. At least we're gonna get a break from some of the severe weather in the northeast.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210924eh940000a\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 CHANGING JOBS DURING THE PANDEMIC REVAMP YOUR RESUME FOR THE CAREER YOU WANT\b0\par\par\uc2 879 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 September 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEFENDING HIS DECISION\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, WITHDRAWAL, AMERICANS, AFGHANS, WAR\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRE, EVACUATIONS, ACRES, LAUCHNER\par \par \uc2 IDA, RAINFALL, FLOODING, TORNADOES\par \par \uc2 IDA, POWER, FLOODING, RESIDENTS, FUEL, EDWARDS\par \par \uc2 TEXAS, ABORTION, SUPREME COURT, VOTING\par \par \uc2 COVID, HOSPITALS, VACCINATIONS, COLLEGES\par \par \uc2 LITTLE LEAGUE, TIGERS, CHAMPIONS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 NEWTON, PATRIOTS, COVID, \uc2 NFL\uc2 , VACCINATION, MEYER\par \par \uc2 "JEOPARDY", RICHARDS, SCHNEIDER, CHU\par \par \uc2 "CANDYMAN," BOX OFFICE, DACOSTA, VICTORIA, RUBIN\par \par \uc2 PARNELL, FORECAST, STORM, TREATS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEAT INDEX FORECAST\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: PRESIDENT BIDEN DEFENDS AFGHANISTAN EXIT\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, \uc2 TALIBAN\uc2 , WAR, WITHDRAWAL, AFGHANISTAN\par \par \uc2 WILDFIRE, CALDOR, CALIFORNIA, EVACUATIONS, LAKE TAHOE\par \par \uc2 IDA, STORM, FLOODING, MID-ATLANTIC, NORTHEAST\par \par \uc2 ALZHEIMER'S, SLEEP, STUDY, STANFORD, LINK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 NEWBORN, TODDLER, PREP, HOME, MARGOLIN, GALLANT\par \par \uc2 JOBS, PANDEMIC, AMAZON, RESUME, LOPEZ, FISHER, CHALULEU\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We're going to take a look now at how to revitalize your resume. A recent report found more than 40 percent of employees are considering making a career change. Rebecca Jarvis here with an exclusive announcement from Amazon that could help you find a new job. Plus, some tips on how to stand out from the competition. Good morning, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good morning, George. Yeah, that resume for many job candidates is going to be the first impression that employers make of you, but in this new virtual environment it may also be the only real look they get into you and your background, so here's how you stand out from the crowd.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Two and a half years ago, Albert Lopez was gearing up to make a career change until a devastating cancer diagnosis got in his way.\par \par \uc2 ALBERT LOPEZ (LOOKING FOR A NEW JOB)\par \par \uc2 It made me a little bit more dependent on the job that I had because of that health insurance.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Thankfully, with proper treatment, Albert is in remission and ready to start his next chapter.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) What are your biggest questions about landing your next job?\par \par \uc2 ALBERT LOPEZ (LOOKING FOR A NEW JOB)\par \par \uc2 How can I use the current experience that I have now towards something that I would want to do related in the music industry?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Albert sent me his resume ahead of time to assess.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I love that on your resume, you put your skills right up top. Are there any skills you considered adding to your resume that you didn't?\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: LANDING YOUR NEXT JOB: WHY EMPHASIZING SKILLS ON YOUR RESUME IS MORE IMPORTANT THAN EVER\par \par \uc2 ALBERT LOPEZ (LOOKING FOR A NEW JOB)\par \par \uc2 I don't know what skills would be applicable to, like, a lot of other different jobs.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) This idea about your strength under pressure, that you're a great communicator, that is an important skill and it's definitely something that a lot of corporations are looking for.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) We discovered skillful communication was a common thread throughout his work experience and something to emphasize.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) When you can put that out there and show employers through your resume that you're really comfortable in multiple settings, that's the kind of thing that they'll know under pressure they can count on, Albert.\par \par \uc2 ALBERT LOPEZ (LOOKING FOR A NEW JOB)\par \par \uc2 I would never have thought to put it that way.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Emphasizing skills on your resume is more important than ever.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER (CAREER EXPERT \uc2 LINKEDIN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 What happened during COVID is companies were having a hard time finding employees. Companies started to look at the employees based on their skills as opposed to, based on industry.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Experts recommend doing a skills inventory.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: ROCK YOUR RESUME: DOING A SKILLS INVENTORY\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER (CAREER EXPERT \uc2 LINKEDIN\uc2 )\par \par \uc2 Take a look at the skills that you have and compare it to the job description, and make sure that you're really highlighting the skills that they're looking for.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) To build your skills, places like \uc2 Linkedin\uc2  are offering free online courses for their 20 most popular classes through October 15th.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AMAZON TO HOST A CAREER FAIR: OFFERING 20,000 FREE ONE-ON-ONE CAREER COACHING SESSIONS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And Amazon tells "Good Morning America" exclusively they will be hosting a career fair on September 15th, offering 20,000 free one-on-one career coaching sessions.\par \par \uc2 LULI CHALULEU (DIRECTOR OF TALENT ACQUISITION WORLDWIDE OPERATIONS AMAZON)\par \par \uc2 You might want some support on your resume, you might want some interview tips about Amazon, or you might just want some generalized career advice, and that's what the recruiters are ready to do.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Again, that career fair through Amazon September 15th. We'll have a lot more information about that coming up. But, Cecilia, the bottom line here is in addition to building up those skills on your resume, include things like your certifications, even your volunteer and hobby experience if it's relevant to the job you are applying to. Cecilia.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All of that helps. Okay, Rebecca, thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210908eh910000n\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 RISING COVID DEATHS HEALTH CARE WORKERS BEING PUSHED TO THE BRINK\b0\par\par\uc2 826 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 August 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: World News Tonight with David Muir\par \uc2 WNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 TALIBAN\uc2 , AFGHANISTAN, EVACUATIONS, BIDEN, SULLIVAN\par \par \uc2 TALIBAN\uc2 , AFGHANISTAN, EVACUATIONS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO LEAVE\par \par \uc2 COVID-19, VACCINES, SAFETY, FAUCI, JONES, DEBROECK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RISING COVID DEATHS\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And we move on tonight, though, to the pandemic in this country. The US now reporting more than 140,000 new cases of COVID a day in this country. Tonight, here, we're inside hospitals, at a breaking point. And Dr. Fauci, this evening, saying the US could get back to some sort of normalcy by next spring, but what he said has to happen before then. And those new numbers just in from the CDC tonight for fully vaccinated Americans, how quickly does the vaccine begin to wear off over time? ABC's Victor Oquendo, from Miami, tonight.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HEALTH CARE WORKERS BEING PUSHED TO THE BRINK\par \par \uc2 VICTOR OQUENDO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Tonight, hospitals in the South buckling under a crush of COVID patients from Houston, where some ERs have closed their doors, to Louisiana, which marked its deadliest day of the pandemic. Inside Willis-Knighton Hospital in Shreveport, exhausted staff facing a seemingly endless loss of life.\par \par \uc2 DENISE JONES (CHIEF NURSING OFFICER WILLIS-KNIGHTON HOSPITAL)\par \par \uc2 Imagine the pressure of knowing that I don't know if I can do this another day, another hour. But if I don't show up tomorrow, there's nobody here to take care of this patient. There's nobody here to hold this phone and let them talk to their family the last time before we put a tube in them.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR OQUENDO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Nursing teams locked in a grim cycle, carefully moving the dead from the hospital to the care of funeral homes, only to go right back to the ICU, where they will race to save more COVID patients like this 36-year-old.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN DEBROECK (WIFE OF COVID-19 PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 It's really hard to see your strong husband so weak.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR OQUENDO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Lauren Debroeck, who's recovering from COVID herself, has waited nearly a month for her husband to wake up. Lauren says he wasn't ready to get the vaccine, and she just never found the time.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN DEBROECK (WIFE OF COVID-19 PATIENT)\par \par \uc2 I made the appointment three times and canceled it, because I was too busy. If I could go back in time, that is the one thing I would change.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR OQUENDO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Still, \uc2 Dr. Anthony Fauci\uc2  says he sees a light at the end of the tunnel, as long as millions more Americans get vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 DR ANTHONY FAUCI\uc2  (CHIEF MEDICAL ADVISER ON COVID-19)\par \par \uc2 If we can get through this winter and get really a majority, overwhelming majority of the 90 million people who have not been vaccinated, vaccinated, I hope we could start to get some good control in the spring of 2022.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID DEATHS SURGING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FAUCI: COULD CONTROL COVID BY 'SPRING 2022'\par \par \uc2 VICTOR OQUENDO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some say the new \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval for the \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  vaccine helped convince them to get the shot.\par \par \uc2 PEDESTRIAN (FEMALE)\par \par \uc2 Now that there are things that are \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved and stuff like that, that just makes it a little bit more comfortable. For me, I did need to see a little bit more research and just a little, some \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval, at the very least.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR OQUENDO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) And for the millions of fully vaccinated, that third shot, a booster shot, just weeks away. Officials say it could be available by mid-September. Now, more evidence Americans will need it.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC: VACCINE EFFICACY DROPPED FROM 91% TO 66%\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CDC STUDY OF FRONTLINE WORKERS\par \par \uc2 VICTOR OQUENDO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) An updated CDC study of 4,000 frontline workers found that vaccine effectiveness at preventing any infection dropped from 91% to 66% from April to August, a drop that was likely fueled by the Delta variant surge and a waning of vaccine efficacy over time, especially for health care workers who were among the first in the nation to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And, Victor, I want to get right back to that study. And when you see the efficacy waning over time, we should also point out that these vaccines are still very effective against severe disease and hospitalizations. But the numbers are driving the argument for that third shot, a booster shot, which federal authorities say could be available by mid-September.\par \par \uc2 VICTOR OQUENDO (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) David, experts stress that while we are seeing more of these mild breakthrough infections, the vaccine still dramatically reduced the risk of hospitalizations or dying of COVID. Now there is evidence of the vaccines waning over time, and that's why health officials will recommend a booster shot eight months after that second shot. David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MUIR (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Victor Oquendo from Miami, tonight. Thank you, Victor.\par \par \uc2 Document WNT0000020210825eh8o00004\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 BREAKING FREE FROM BURNOUT TAKING CARE OF YOUR FAMILY & YOURSELF IN STRESSFUL TIMES\b0\par\par\uc2 1,580 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE OF EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 RAINFALL, FLOODING, MISSING, SEARCH\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, RAINFALL, HENRI, THUNDERSTORMS\par \par \uc2 HENRI, RAIN, WINDS, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 AFGHANISTAN, EVACUATE, BIDEN, \uc2 NATO\uc2 , \uc2 TALIBAN\par \par \uc2 PENTAGON, EVACUEES, AIRLINES, TRANSPORT\par \par \uc2 COVID, CASES, FDA, \uc2 PFIZER\uc2 , APPROVAL\par \par \uc2 EVERLY, DUO, PIONEER, TRIBUTES\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 "JEOPARDY!", RICHARDS, PODCAST, MCNEAR, HACKETT, HOST\par \par \uc2 YOSEMITE, BRIESE, GERRISH, CHUNG, HIKING\par \par \uc2 CRUISES, COVID, CDC, GUIDANCE, MCDANIEL\par \par \uc2 JONES, NO HITTER, LITTLE LEAGUE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FIRE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, SEARCH, RESCUE, BOOTH\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: AT LEAST 21 KILLED IN TENNESSEE FLOODING DISASTER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: URGENT SEARCH FOR 20 PEOPLE STILL MISSING\par \par \uc2 TROPICAL STORM HENRI, POWER OUTAGE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HENRI WREAKS HAVOC\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THOUSANDS LEFT WITHOUT POWER AFTER TROPICAL STORM MAKES LANDFALL\par \par \uc2 TALIBAN\uc2 , AFGHANISTAN, BIDEN, EVACUATIONS\par \par \uc2 BEYONCE, JAY-Z, TIFFANY, AEROSMITH, SHIELDS\par \par \uc2 LORENZ, VACCINES, BRANDY, MURPHY, RODRIGO, ZEILER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GMA COVER STORY\par \par \uc2 COVID, PARENTING, BURNOUT, ANDERSON, SOUTER, QUINTANA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "THE PANDEMIC PARENT TRAP"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BREAKING FREE FROM BURNOUT\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TAKING CARE OF YOUR FAMILY & YOURSELF IN STRESSFUL TIMES\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Now to our new series, "The Pandemic Parent Trap," helping you take care of your family and yourself during stressful times.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) At one point of the pandemic, 66% of moms say they had to balance child care and working from home. And our Janai Norman is back with some moms feeling that pressure. Good morning again, Janai.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) T.J., moms feeling the pressure, I don't know why they put me on this story. When it comes to dealing with the daily demands, along with child care and work, that's the way of life for parents even without a pandemic. But you add that in the mix, and you have upped your parental stress levels exponentially.\par \par \uc2 TOMIKA ANDERSON (MOM)\par \par \uc2 Okay, you've got to get everything together for school. It's about to start. So, here's all the school supplies.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's all in a day's work for Tomika Anderson.\par \par \uc2 TOMIKA ANDERSON (MOM)\par \par \uc2 As the kids like to say, mommy does the most. In addition to being a mom, I run a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  community. I have a full-time job. I have three businesses. So, I'm exhausted.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The single mom taking on more work to afford child care, which comes at a cost.\par \par \uc2 TOMIKA ANDERSON (MOM)\par \par \uc2 As a single parent, you know, you're the one who's got this. And if you're the one who's got this, you've got to continually think about how to do this without falling apart.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Mercedes Quintana also feeling the pressure.\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES QUINTANA (MOM)\par \par \uc2 Parenting during a pandemic is 100 times more stressful. We just are so exhausted physically, emotionally.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Seventeen months into the pandemic, Mercedes says it's a daily juggle to balance working from home with child care.\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES QUINTANA (MOM)\par \par \uc2 You're stuck having to figure it out on your own 24/7. But you're still expected to meet the same expectations.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The pandemic taking a toll on moms all across the country. And as more uncertainty lies ahead, for Tomika and Mercedes, it's about taking it one day at a time.\par \par \uc2 MERCEDES QUINTANA (MOM)\par \par \uc2 I've learned to give myself grace and breaks, learning to work through all that burnout.\par \par \uc2 JANAI NORMAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Give yourself grace. Parents have got to find a way to prioritize themselves along with everything else. I always tell myself, you can't pour from an empty cup. And, guys, I'm ready for this parental stress test. Finally, a test I know I'll ace.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right, well, stay tuned here because we have our friend and parenting expert Ericka Souter just about to release a book. Congrats on that. It's "How to Have A Kid and A Life: A Survival Guide." That sounds a little scary but necessary. But tell us, first of all, look, parents feel burnt out all the time. So, we have a test now that can help you understand whether or not you really need some help.\par \par \uc2 ERICKA SOUTER (AUTHOR "HOW TO HAVE A KID AND A LIFE: A SURVIVAL GUIDE")\par \par \uc2 Right. Even before the pandemic, parenting was hard. Now, it's completely overwhelming. So, you have to kind of figure out what it is you need to feel better. So, I have this mommy burnout litmus test. But dads can take it, too.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE MOMMY BURNOUT LITMUS TEST\par \par \uc2 ERICKA SOUTER (AUTHOR "HOW TO HAVE A KID AND A LIFE: A SURVIVAL GUIDE")\par \par \uc2 All right? So, first, you wanna ask yourself, am I nurturing my marriage or partnership? Number two, is my career going in the right direction? If that's applicable. Number three, do I have supportive friends to turn to? People who'll listen to me, who care about me. Number three, am I nurturing those passions or interests I had before kids? And lastly, do I have an outlet that's just for me, not for anyone else in my family? Now, if you answered no to a lot of those questions, it's time to reprioritize.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Wow.\par \par \uc2 ERICKA SOUTER (AUTHOR "HOW TO HAVE A KID AND A LIFE: A SURVIVAL GUIDE")\par \par \uc2 And, you know, that's counterintuitive with what's going on. But there is no grand prize for being a mommy martyr who does nothing for herself. And, in fact, if you're not feeling nurtured and supported, you're probably not being the parent and partner that you want to be.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah, you could say that again. Okay. So, you're Janai, you aced the test. You have burnout. You officially tested positive for burnout. What's the first step? Especially with the school year just about to start.\par \par \uc2 ERICKA SOUTER (AUTHOR "HOW TO HAVE A KID AND A LIFE: A SURVIVAL GUIDE")\par \par \uc2 Yeah. In these uncertain times right now, it's really important for parents to be upfront and honest with themselves and their children.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: DEALING WITH BURNOUT\par \par \uc2 ERICKA SOUTER (AUTHOR "HOW TO HAVE A KID AND A LIFE: A SURVIVAL GUIDE")\par \par \uc2 Going into the school year, it may not look like anyone expects it to look. There could be mask mandates, there could be social distancing mandates, a lot of activities could be canceled. So, you wanna talk to your kids about that. You also wanna know the school's COVID plan. If a kid tests positive, what does that mean? Is everyone sent home? And for how long? You also wanna have a back-up plan, right? If schools are closed and everyone is at home again, what does that look like? Do you have sitters or family members or neighbors that you can lean on? Have you talked to your employer about being home or taking or having adjusted hours? You really need to prepare for what that's gonna look like.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Being a better parent we hit on here. You have to be good to yourself. But what are ways parents can be better to themselves?\par \par \uc2 ERICKA SOUTER (AUTHOR "HOW TO HAVE A KID AND A LIFE: A SURVIVAL GUIDE")\par \par \uc2 Well, you have to make time for yourself. I'm not talking a lot of time. It could be 15 minutes of yoga. It could be 15 minutes of running, watching a reality show, which is what I love to do, or talking to your best friend every night. It's about consistency. It's about looking forward to something that is just for you to take that edge off. And, you know, forget about those anxiety and fears that cloak so much of our day.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, how do you teach your kids this important lesson? Because we have to learn it ourselves, but we also have to teach our kids to take care of themselves and ourselves as well.\par \par \uc2 ERICKA SOUTER (AUTHOR "HOW TO HAVE A KID AND A LIFE: A SURVIVAL GUIDE")\par \par \uc2 Yes. Kids need to know how to take care of themselves, but they also have to know how to take care of their families. We are families. We're in this together. So, kids as young as 4 can participate and contribute. Clean up messes, help with laundry, help set the table. Kids and families need to know we're all in this together.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Ericka, it so good to have you back here in the studio.\par \par \uc2 ERICKA SOUTER (AUTHOR "HOW TO HAVE A KID AND A LIFE: A SURVIVAL GUIDE")\par \par \uc2 It's good to be back.\par \par \uc2 T.J. HOLMES (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good to see you. And the book, congrats again. It's "How to Have A Kid and A Life: A Survival Guide," it is out tomorrow. And for more help on helping yourself and your family, you can head over to our website.\par \par \uc2 CECILIA VEGA (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Very excited about all of that. Congratulations again. Coming up, everybody, John Cena. He's right here in the studio. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STORY SYNOPSES\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210909eh8n0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 SUMMER JOB MARKET TEENS FILL EMPLOYMENT VOID AS BUSINESSES REOPEN\b0\par\par\uc2 643 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 July 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COVID CASES SOARING\par \par \uc2 COVID, UNVACCINATED, PANDEMIC, WALENSKY, CDC, GISE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: CORONAVIRUS EMERGENCY, THE LATEST: US DAILY CASES UP NEARLY 70%\par \par \uc2 DELTA, VARIANT, OFFIT\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, OLYMPICS, ATHLETES, COVID\par \par \uc2 HEAT, DROUGHT\par \par \uc2 FLOODING, EUROPE\par \par \uc2 DACA, ILLEGAL, DREAMERS\par \par \uc2 CUOMO, ALLEGATIONS, INVESTIGATION\par \par \uc2 FIRE\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , \uc2 SOCIAL JUSTICE COALITION\uc2 , POLICE REFORM\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2 , FINALS\par \par \uc2 SPEARS, LEGAL, CONSERVATORSHIP\par \par \uc2 MARKIE, HIP HOP\par \par \uc2 TEENS, JOB, MARKET, EMPLOYMENT\par \par \uc2 TERRELL BROWN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Welcome back to "GMA" on a Saturday. In the boom for teenagers in this recovering job market, many businesses are relying on the younger set to get them through to better times. ABC's Deirdre Bolton has more.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Restaurants, amusement parks, retail shops, they all need workers.\par \par \uc2 BUSINESS OWNER (MALE)\par \par \uc2 Without them it's, we wouldn't be able to open our doors.\par \par \uc2 LYN JAMES (BUSINESS OWNER FLOWERS & CAPPUCCINO CAFE)\par \par \uc2 This summer we've seen the largest number of teens employed here.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Economists saying this is the best summer in decades for American teenagers looking for work.\par \par \uc2 LUKE PARDUE (ECONOMIST GUSTO)\par \par \uc2 For almost 50 years we've been seeing this continual decline in the portion of teenagers taking summer jobs. And like so many things over the past year, this pandemic has turned that trend up on its head.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The economy has rebounded much faster than many expected and some businesses are overwhelmed by pent-up demand.\par \par \uc2 PROF ALICIA MODESTINO (ECONOMICS PROF NORTHEASTERN UNIVERSITY)\par \par \uc2 As we're reopening restaurants, hotels, retail stores, there's a sense among employers that they don't wanna leave any money on the table this summer because they've suffered so much over the last 18 months.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Some adults are still wrestling with childcare issues or concerns about their health or the health of someone they live with.\par \par \uc2 LUKE PARDUE (ECONOMIST GUSTO)\par \par \uc2 Teens have stepped in to fill this gap - while older workers remain either unwilling or unable to enter the workforce.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For teens, greater demand equals higher wages.\par \par \uc2 JOCELYN KULSETH (YOUTH EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 I get paid well and then with tips too, that's always really nice.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER\par \par \uc2 (VO) Employers like Alonso Soliz, who runs a Tropical Smoothie franchise, are competing for workers. And even small businesses feel pressure to add benefits.\par \par \uc2 ALONZO SOLIZ (BUSINESS OWNER TROPICAL SMOOTHIE CAFE)\par \par \uc2 I give the kids free food at times. I'm trying to see what I can do to actually, to get these kids to stay, these, these teenagers. Another thing that I've been doing is tuition reimbursement for people that are working 30 hours or more. I'm trying to do everything I can to keep employees.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) For teenagers, many seem happy about making money and being sought after and for the work experience.\par \par \uc2 LEXI GUEBARA (YOUTH EMPLOYEE)\par \par \uc2 Yeah, so I'm a shift lead and I'm just making smoothies, making food. I have to close the store. I'm very thankful to have a job and a good job.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) As for what teens in the workforce means for the economy, most experts agree, it's a net positive.\par \par \uc2 LUKE PARDUE (ECONOMIST GUSTO)\par \par \uc2 We can speed up the recovery while also making sure that teenagers are earning a paycheck.\par \par \uc2 DEIRDRE BOLTON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Once late August comes, some small businesses may feel the crunch as older teens go to college. Whit.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) All right. Deirdre Bolton for us. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 WHIT JOHNSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Coming up here on "Good Morning America," Billie Jean King and the others being honored for changing the way prize money is awarded in women's tennis. The interviews you'll see only here on "GMA."\par \par \uc2 COMMERCIAL BREAK\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210726eh7h00036\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 "WORK FROM HOME" BATTLES BREWING COMPANIES & EMPLOYEES STRUGGLING WITH RETURN TO OFFICE\b0\par\par\uc2 1,002 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 July 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BRACING FOR IMPACT\par \par \uc2 ELSA, LANDFALL, RAIN, WINDS, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 ELSA, RAIN, WINDS, LANDFALL\par \par \uc2 COLLAPSE, DEBRIS, SEARCH, FLORIDA\par \par \uc2 COVID, CASES, VARIANT, BIDEN, VACCINATED, SHUFORD\par \par \uc2 JHA, VARIANTS, VACCINATIONS, EFFECTIVENESS\par \par \uc2 ASSASSINATION, MOISE, ASSAILANTS, HAITI\par \par \uc2 PLOT, FOILED, ARSENAL, ARREST, CASTEEL\par \par \uc2 NBA\uc2  FINALS, SUNS, BUCKS, PAUL, ANTETOKOUNMPO\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TIMING ELSA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 JARAMILLO, AMUSEMENT PARK, RAFT, OVERTURNED\par \par \uc2 RICHARDSON, OLYMPICS, TRACK & FIELD, THC\par \par \uc2 SPEARS, CONSERVATORSHIP, INGHAM, QUIT, ATTORNEY\par \par \uc2 SERENADE, ANNIVERSARY\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: STATE OF EMERGENCY\par \par \uc2 ELSA, RAIN, WINDS, FLOODING\par \par \uc2 COVID, VARIANT, CASES, BIDEN, FREDERICK, JHA, \uc2 DELTA\par \par \uc2 CANNES, LEE, PICASSO, HOT DOGS\par \par \uc2 WORK, HOME, COMPANIES, EMPLOYEES, HYBRID\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Ponder it all day long. But for now, we're going to go on to our "GMA" cover story, and it's the work from home debate. Front page of "The Wall Street Journal" took it on this morning. So many people here discussing with offices around the country reopening, do you need to return and how to talk to your employer about what's right for you. Our chief business correspondent Rebecca Jarvis here to break it down. Good morning, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: "WORK FROM HOME" BATTLES BREWING\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: COMPANIES & EMPLOYEES STRUGGLING WITH HOW TO HANDLE RETURN TO THE OFFICE\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good morning, George. Yeah, and this is the question, so many CEOs I speak to, workers I speak to are asking, what's it going to look like in the fall? Do we have to go back? Will there be a hybrid model? And depending on the company and industry, things are looking very different. So, for example, if you work in computers with IT and big tech, a lot of those companies are saying you can work remotely. \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  have notably told most of their employees they can do things remotely.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) If you happen to work in banking and you work for \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  or \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , two of the biggest companies in that industry, they say they want their employees back as much as five days a week. They want them in their seats and interacting with clients. Flex Jobs took a look at all the industries out there and they found that the most likely industries to offer remote work are in customer service, computer and IT, education and marketing, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And how about if your employer is somewhere in the middle and they're sort of leaving it up to you? How do you negotiate whether to stay at home or go into the office?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) A lot of people are going to be in this in between situation, George, and how you play it can really affect and impact the outcome for you and your family. First thing you want to do is look at the company policy. Does the company offer remote work? If they do, you're in luck. That makes it a much easier conversation. If they don't, you have to really prepare for a more complicated situation. You want to, after you look at the policy, look at your work. What is your job specifically? What are the hours you work. What's your job function? How do you communicate with the rest of the employees including your manager?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Put together a plan that could work for remote work for you. Then you want to meet with your manager. Set that meeting up well in advance, be prepared with your sheet of paper ready to go talking through the points. And then this is not about convincing your boss that you need to work from home, this is about convincing your boss that you working from home is going to work best for the company. Maybe you have a two hour commute and you can work during that two hour commute. And you have to finally, George, be prepared to hear no. If you hear no, you have a couple of options, one, you can offer to do this temporarily, maybe give it a try, let's see how one month goes and see if that works or not. Two, you might have to be willing to leave your job for something else if work from home is not a possibility at your company, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And we are seeing people do that. We're also seeing so many people who've gotten used to working at home, like the convenience of it. But what are the downsides?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Sure, well, working from home can be great. It really works for a lot of people. But a lot of companies that are now offering work from home say you still have to be in a specific city, state or geography, country, location, that can make an impact. Also working from home policies, they are subject to change. And finally, George, guys, these things, face time is always important when it comes to a workplace. Make sure you're communicating still with your managers and the people who will give you promotions in the future.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Rebecca Jarvis, thanks very much. Important advice.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Can I also add, you know, I was working from home for seven months. I have a new appreciation for people who work from home. Yeah, there is a convenience there. It is work. It is, I mean, no, seriously, people think, oh, you're at home, you're always in your PJs, which, you know, that is a benefit of it, but in all sincerity it is not...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Just from the waist down.\par \par \uc2 ROBIN ROBERTS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah. And can we shout out to our producers and editors who are still working at home and - putting us on the air.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yes.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210716eh770000k\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 WEIGHING THE PANDEMIC'S IMPACT ON WORKING MOMS WOMEN-LED SECTORS HIT HARD BY COVID SHUTDOWNS\b0\par\par\uc2 591 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, GOP, CHENEY, STEFANIK, VOTING, 2020, AUDIT\par \par \uc2 TRUMP, GOP, CHENEY, VOTING, COVID, JOBS, BIDEN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 INDIA, COVID, VOLUNTEERS, OXYGEN, DEATHS, VACCINES\par \par \uc2 FAUCI, INDIA, COVID, BIDEN, BEDS, VACCINE, MASKS\par \par \uc2 SILVER, TRUMP, SOCIAL MEDIA, BAN, FIVETHIRTYEIGHT\par \par \uc2 PANDEMIC, MOMS, WOMEN, WORKFORCE, INEQUALITY\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (UNITED STATES)\par \par \uc2 About two-and-a-half million women have dropped out or have been forced out of the workforce during the pandemic. Our economy cannot fully recover unless women can participate, fully. Women leaving the work force in these numbers, it's a national emergency, and it demands a national solution.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WORSE FINANCIAL CIRCUMSTANCES\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  weighing in on the exodus from, of women from the workforce during this pandemic. Friday's jobs numbers did not meet expectations, and the economic impact on women is coming into sharper focus on this Mother's Day. In our latest poll, 25% of women say they are worse off financially than they were a year ago, compared to 18% of men. Economics correspondent Rebecca Jarvis explains how working moms are bearing the brunt of the COVID shutdowns.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WEIGHING THE PANDEMIC'S IMPACT ON WORKING MOMS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Today, as we celebrate mothers, a reality check on the challenges facing working moms, like Raena Boston, who, just after earning a promotion at her HR job, had a difficult choice to make when the pandemic shutdowns began.\par \par \uc2 RAENA BOSTON (WORKING MOM)\par \par \uc2 If I had to decide between staying at home or taking a step back, career-wise, I decided to take a step back. That was what was gonna be best for my family.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: WOMEN-LED SECTORS HIT HARD BY COVID SHUTDOWNS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: UNEMPLOYMENT AMONG US WOMEN\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) After a year of loss, the pandemic ravaging the service sector, restaurants, hotels, clothing shops where women, particularly women of color hold a majority of jobs, and hospitals where women make up 77% of workers, the early signs of a recovery. But, even though hiring is picking up and unemployment among women is ticking down, now 5.6%, the headlines masking another trend.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW HAS COVID WORSENED GENDER INEQUALITY?\par \par \uc2 KARIN KIMBROUGH (LINKED IN CHIEF ECONOMIST)\par \par \uc2 The problem is that, as women decide that they need to stay home, either to take care of family members, particularly children who aren't in school, they are not going to be looking for jobs, and they won't be reflected in the unemployment statistics.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 2.4 MILLION WOMEN LEFT WORKFORCE\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: BETWEEN FEBRUARY 2020-FEBRUARY 2021\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Now, more than 2 million women have dropped out of the workforce. Another 165,000 left just last month, an untold number still struggling with the balance.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: HOW CAN EMPLOYERS BETTER SUPPORT WORKING PARENTS?\par \par \uc2 WHITNEY RAMON (WORKING MOM)\par \par \uc2 I felt like there was not enough time in the day for me to meet the - needs of my children, and my own needs and my professional needs.\par \par \uc2 KARIN KIMBROUGH (LINKED IN CHIEF ECONOMIST)\par \par \uc2 And since we know that women are responsible for two-thirds of work around the house, they were more likely to shoulder this responsibility.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) To fully recover the remaining 8.2 million jobs lost to the pandemic, everyone, women and men, needs to get back to work.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Thanks to Rebecca for that.\par \par \uc2 Document THWK000020210511eh5900007\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 HOW TO GET THE JOB YOU WANT NEW ONLINE COURSES TO SHARPEN \uc2 IN-DEMAND\uc2  SKILLS\b0\par\par\uc2 734 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 April 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: Good Morning America\par \uc2 GMA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021, American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO VACCINATE\par \par \uc2 COVID, VACCINE, BIDEN, CASES, FAUCI, MODERNA\par \par \uc2 TRIAL, CHAUVIN, TESTIMONY, PROSECUTION, DEFENSE\par \par \uc2 SHOOTING, NAVY, INJURIES, SAILORS, SUSPECT\par \par \uc2 BORDER, MIGRANT, ABANDONED, CHILD\par \par \uc2 RESCUE, CARGO SHIP, SINKING, CREW, ARSET\par \par \uc2 "FORBES", BILLIONAIRES, KARDASHIAN, DOLAN, BEZOS\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: SEVERE WEATHER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: GOOD MORNING AMERICA\par \par \uc2 GAETZ, PARDON, TRUMP, INVESTIGATION, SEX TRAFFICKING\par \par \uc2 VACCINE, PASSPORT, ABBOTT, PRIVACY, MCALPIN, BAN\par \par \uc2 PASSPORT, BUSINESSES, VACCINE, STATES, CRUISES\par \par \uc2 BRADY, SUPER BOWL, BUCS, PATRIOTS, ROOKIE CARD\par \par \uc2 ELLIE, "TOTAL ECLIPSE OF THE HEART"\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: FLOODING RAIN\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: RACE TO VACCINATE\par \par \uc2 COVID, VACCINE, VARIANTS, MICHIGAN\par \par \uc2 CHAUVIN, FLOYD, TRIAL, MERCIL, PROSECUTION, OFFICERS\par \par \uc2 MLB, FOUL BALL, CAMERA\par \par \uc2 ROBERTS, CLOONEY, "CRUELLA," DIAMOND, OLD NAVY, GARDNER\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: JULIA ROBERTS AND GEORGE CLOONEY TEAMING BACK UP FOR ROMCOM\par \par \uc2 BRADY, SUPER BOWL, FAMILY, QB, MARRIAGE\par \par \uc2 WOMEN, MENOPAUSE, STUDY, AGE\par \par \uc2 HARRY, MARKLE, \uc2 NETFLIX\uc2 , PROJECT, REID\par \par \uc2 "THUNDER FORCE"\par \par \uc2 LINKEDIN\uc2 , KATCHENAGO, JOB, \uc2 MICROSOFT\uc2 , FISHER\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We are back with a new way to get the job you really want. The employment social platform \uc2 Linkedin\uc2  is now offering classes to help job hunters sharpen the skills employers are looking for. Rebecca Jarvis is back with more on this new program. Hey, Rebecca.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Hey, George. And the good news here is that the pace of hiring is picking up. There are 7.4 million job openings right now. And employers, the trick here is to find the skills that they are looking for and to have those skills. And these free courses are a way that you can gain them and fast track yourself to landing your dream job.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Rachelle Katchenago is a hard-working mom of two who recently found herself at a crossroads.\par \par \uc2 RACHELLE KATCHENAGO (JOB SEEKER)\par \par \uc2 I was working in factory work. Mostly it turned out to be either temporary or dead end. So after another temp job ended, I found myself unemployed. Like, how am I going to pay the bills for me and my family?\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) So Rachelle started looking for a way to pivot and that's when she discovered \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's Global Skills Initiative that allowed her to gain new certifications through free online courses on \uc2 Linkedin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RACHELLE KATCHENAGO (JOB SEEKER)\par \par \uc2 What I ended up doing is taking the sales and the customer service at the same time, so I jammed a lot into a small period of time. And I landed some certificates which helped me in my job search.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: 10 MOST \uc2 IN-DEMAND\uc2  CAREERS\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) The different learning courses help job seekers qualify for the ten most in-demand careers including customer service specialist, project manager, data analysis, sales development representative, and software developer.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: \uc2 MICROSOFT\uc2 /LINKEDIN COMMITMENT TO HELP 250,000 COMPANIES\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) Plus, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  along with \uc2 Linkedin\uc2  just announced a commitment to help 250,000 companies like Gap and \uc2 TaskRabbit\uc2  make hires based on skills over other qualifications.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE FISHER (\uc2 LINKEDIN\uc2  CAREER EXPERT)\par \par \uc2 The industries that are hiring like crazy, they can't find enough people. It's because they've been looking the wrong way. Many people who have been laid off during this pandemic have the skills needed for these job openings, so it's really looking at the skills as opposed to education or the network, more those traditional ways of seeking out candidates.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (VO) It's how Rachelle was able to land a new full time job as a customer service representative.\par \par \uc2 RACHELLE KATCHENAGO (JOB SEEKER)\par \par \uc2 Well, now that I have this full time job it's like a big, a big relief, like now I, I actually made it. I can, I can be content with life.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA JARVIS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well done, Rachelle. And you can get started too. You need to start out by having a \uc2 Linkedin\uc2  account. You can make one for free. And then once you're there search \uc2 Linkedin\uc2  Learning. It will ask you a few questions about the types of things you're interested in learning about and the types of jobs you're interested in seeking. And then, Michael, you're on your way to earning those certificates and landing your dream job. Michael.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STRAHAN (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Rebecca, thank you, as always. Always helping so many people out there. Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Document GMA0000020210423eh470000r\par }\page {\par\fs20\b \uc2 THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\b0\par\par\uc2 4,745 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 February 2021\par \uc2 ABC News: This Week\par \uc2 THWK\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021 American Broadcasting Companies, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THIS WEEK\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, BILL, TRUMP, CPAC, GOP, SAUDI, PRINCE, IRAN\par \par \uc2 HIRONO, BIDEN, SAUDI, BILL, CUOMO, HATE CRIMES\par \par \uc2 PORTMAN, GOP, BIDEN, POLLS, SANCTIONS, TRUMP, CHIP\par \par \uc2 FAUCI, FDA, VACCINE, J&J, \uc2 PFIZER\uc2 , MODERNA, EQUITY\par \par \uc2 BIDEN, COVID, WAGE, MANCHIN, TRUMP, CPAC, CUOMO\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RICK SCOTT (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 We will not win the future by trying to go back to where the Republican Party used to be.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TED CRUZ (REP) (TEXAS)\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you this right now, \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  ain't going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 RON DESANTIS\uc2  (REP) (FLORIDA)\par \par \uc2 We will not go back to the days of the failed Republican establishment of yesteryear.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (REP-CA) (MINORITY LEADER)\par \par \uc2 They said we were going to lose 20 seats that night. No one said we'd win seats. You know why we won that? President Trump worked on all these races.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: THE POWERHOUSE ROUNDTABLE\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Still Donald Trump's party. The CPAC conference, we're getting ready to see him today. We're going to talk about that on our Roundtable joined by \uc2 Rahm Emanuel\uc2 , \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , the CEO of Democracy for America, Yvette Simpson, and Republican strategist Alice Stewart. Now, he is the former president, so we're going to get to him later. Rahm, let's begin with President Biden and his COVID package. We saw the difficulties he's facing right now in the Senate. You have senators like Mazie Hirono saying minimum wage has to be in the bill. You lose one Democratic senator, it goes down. How does he solve that problem?\par \par \uc2 RAHM EMANUEL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, first of all, I think that Democrats will support this package because it, it has a lot in it that is very good both for the COVID and the economy. I actually think the minimum wage if it's not in it, is an opportunity, and that is you can both get...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) It can't be in it, can it?\par \par \uc2 RAHM EMANUEL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) No, I don't, I think the parliamentarian's pretty clear and the White House is pretty clear they're going to live with that ruling. But it's an opportunity to build a bipartisanship coalition, taking the minimum wage, earned income tax credit, \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 's idea of the child credit, and build a coalition around fighting poverty. And I actually think you will, the, and I think the President will have, and this is also a challenge to Republicans, if you think COVID's out of control and the economy's bad, a vote no is going to look okay. But if you think you're getting COVID under control and the economy is coming back, then a no vote on a very popular package is going to come back to haunt you in 18 months, politically.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yvette...\par \par \uc2 RAHM EMANUEL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And so, I do think that this is an opportunity to get the minimum wage and get his entire package done.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But if you do it somewhere else, is that good enough for progressives?\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) I don't think so. You have to do it here. And I think what we're, we're realizing is that this is a very, very popular position, $15 minimum wage.\par \par \uc2 RAHM EMANUEL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Yeah.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) And if you don't attach it to this package, you're going to compromise it away. We're already seeing Republicans say let's start at $10, let's start work our way up, when we know that this number is already really low compared to what it should be based on inflation. And so, you put it on, in the bill, you whip your caucus. Joe Biden is saying he...\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But Joe Manchin has already said he's not going to do it.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Joe Biden came in on the promise that he can bring Republicans in. If he can't control his caucus, we're in trouble. He needs to do whatever it takes. And guess what? If Manchin is bold enough to vote down the entire bill with $15 an hour, then he, he needs to be ready to take the heat.\par \par \uc2 ALICE STEWART (REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST/CNN)\par \par \uc2 But why...\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) People in West Virginia need this $15 an hour. They need this COVID package.\par \par \uc2 ALICE STEWART (REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST/CNN)\par \par \uc2 Why are we worried about compromising away $15 minimum wage? That is a policy that Progressives have wanted for a long time, and it's not a timely one. COVID is a timely issue. We need this now. We need it timely, and we need it targeted.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) We need it all of it.\par \par \uc2 ALICE STEWART (REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST/CNN)\par \par \uc2 And Democrats have taken a, a nonpartisan issue that is important to the health and the economy of this country and made it a partisan issue. And so, Joe Biden campaigning on being a moderate, has, has put a partisanship in an issue that is timely and very critical to the American people.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Perhaps that holds on minimum wage, but, but \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2  and I have talked to Senator Portman about this, as well. The - the provisions inside this COVID relief bill are popular with Republican voters.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Well, listen, yes, about 40%. Right? So, it's not a majority of Republican voters, for sure. But I've known Joe Manchin for 12 years. He's not voting for it. It's over. He's not, well, good...\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Good riddance.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) You know, this is what I love, Yvette. Because then, Joe Manchin is literally the only standing, living Democrat left in West Virginia, so you want to treat Joe Manchin that way, believe me, we'll welcome him and to the Republican Party and...\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSO