nesses to raise the wage.\par \par \uc2 RAHM EMANUEL (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Here's the one thing that people gotta remember, every one of the Republican leaders voted for the 2007 minimum wage that George Bush signed into law, with small business tax cuts. It can be done. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  voted for it. Roy blunt voted for it. The rest of the Republican leadership in both the House and the Senate that were there in '07 voted for that package. So, there's a, actually a roadmap that is not hard. And George Bush signed it into law.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Let's talk about the Republican Party and CPAC. \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , history seems to be repeating itself again. Three weeks ago, you had \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  saying the President bears responsibility for the siege, Kevin McCarthy says the same thing. Kevin McCarthy is at CPAC saying this is Trump's Republican Party. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  says he'd vote for him in 2024.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Right. I, see, let's start with the McConnell comment. To me, that's not groundbreaking. McConnell has supported every Republican nominee for president. And when he's asked that question, he says, "Well, if Trump's the Republican nominee..." He didn't say he wanted Trump to be the Republican nominee. He said if Trump was the Republican nominee, he would vote for Trump. And so, that to me, wasn't groundbreaking at all.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) The, the bottom line is this, George. You know, Donald Trump is not a departing two-term president like \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  was. And as a result, he's got an opportunity to come back again if he wants to. And there are going to be some in the party who want him, and there are going to be some in the party who don't. But what is consistent is many of the policies that were pursued over the last four years, take aside the personality, take aside the tweets, a lot of those policies are things that Republicans support.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CHRISTIE (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) So, this is going to take some time. I, I know everyone's anxious to have a resolution of this issue. We are literally 40 days since he left office, so let's all take a deep breath. The party will work its way through. And when Joe Biden and these, and, and the Democrats start to - to put policies forward, there'll be things for Republicans to oppose as, as a nonpartisan party.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) But, the, the Trump presidency was never really about the policies.\par \par \uc2 YVETTE SIMPSON (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) No.\par \par \uc2 ALICE STEWART (REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST/CNN)\par \par \uc2 No, it was about the personality. And, and look, while he is certainly a defeated candidate, he is by no doubt the de facto leader of the Republican Party, and certainly at CPAC.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE STEPHANOPOULOS (ABC NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 OC\uc2 ) Is that good for Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 ALICE STEWART (REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST/CNN)\par \par \uc2 It is in terms of his base and keeping them engaged and onboard, and we'll see that today. From his speech today, he will really focus on the policies. He will focus on Conservative principles, limited government and, and individual liberties. But from what I'm hearing, he's going to really lean in on appealing to working Americans across this country, every race, every creed and every color. Now, that is good language, but he needs to follow that up with action and, and that will be important.\par \par \uc2 GRAPHICS: TRUMP TO MAKE FIRST POST-PRESIDENCY SPEECH AT CPAC\par \par \uc2 ALICE STEWART (REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST/CNN)\par \par \uc2 But the key is for, for not to focus on the personality, but to focus on the policies. And those who are - upset with his tone and tenor, they weren'